- Even if I let you in, you couldn't talk

257
00:09:46,552 --> 00:09:48,596
with the Lieutenant now, he's busy.

258
00:09:48,596 --> 00:09:49,483
- [Crow] Nice shoes.

259
00:09:49,483 --> 00:09:50,316
- Alright.

260
00:09:51,563 --> 00:09:55,197
- [Crow] (laughs) Little jerk.

261
00:09:55,197 --> 00:09:57,816
- [Joel] Wow, jeepers, hot dog!

262
00:09:57,816 --> 00:09:58,812
- [Crow] And everywhere,

263
00:09:58,812 --> 00:10:01,377
the smell of men enjoying themselves.

264
00:10:01,377 --> 00:10:02,210
(sniffs)

265
00:10:02,210 --> 00:10:03,043
Oh, oh!

266
00:10:03,043 --> 00:10:05,374
(coughs)

267
00:10:05,374 --> 00:10:06,991
- [Joel] Uh-oh.

268
00:10:06,991 --> 00:10:07,845
- [Crow] Oh, I'll show them.

269
00:10:07,845 --> 00:10:09,432
I'm gonna jump off.

270
00:10:09,432 --> 00:10:11,432
- [Tom] No, don't do it!

271
00:10:12,483 --> 00:10:16,145
- [Joel] And now, back
to man with little pants.

272
00:10:16,145 --> 00:10:18,228
(grunts)

273
00:10:20,212 --> 00:10:24,461
- [Joel] ♫ Oh she came in
though the bathroom window. ♫

274
00:10:24,461 --> 00:10:26,544
(grunts)

275
00:10:27,664 --> 00:10:30,914
- [Crow] Uh-oh, he's having an episode.

276
00:10:31,911 --> 00:10:32,744
- Good work, Jimmy!

277
00:10:32,744 --> 00:10:33,648
- [Tom] Jimmy Smits.

278
00:10:33,648 --> 00:10:34,481
- Don't let him throw ya.

279
00:10:34,481 --> 00:10:35,873
- [Joel] Give him a wedgie!

280
00:10:35,873 --> 00:10:39,063
- [Tom] Purple Herman, woo, wet willy!

281
00:10:39,063 --> 00:10:41,175
- [Crow] It was.

282
00:10:41,175 --> 00:10:43,258
(grunts)

283
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:49,033
Let's go down the slide like this, wee!

284
00:10:49,033 --> 00:10:49,950
- Hold him!

285
00:10:50,888 --> 00:10:53,093
- [Tom] You know there's
something sick about this, guys.

286
00:10:53,093 --> 00:10:55,078
- [Joel] Yeah, I call it history, Tom.

287
00:10:55,078 --> 00:10:57,216
- [Crow] Victory at sea.

288
00:10:57,216 --> 00:10:58,049
- Now you got him, Crash.

289
00:10:58,049 --> 00:10:59,505
Give him the airplane stand.

290
00:10:59,505 --> 00:11:00,562
- [Joel] Good idea, kid!

291
00:11:00,562 --> 00:11:02,237
Why didn't I think of that?

292
00:11:02,237 --> 00:11:05,052
(dramatic music)

293
00:11:05,052 --> 00:11:05,885
- Billy!

294
00:11:05,885 --> 00:11:07,811
- [Tom] Don't be a hero!

295
00:11:07,811 --> 00:11:09,094
- [Crash] Hold tight!

296
00:11:09,094 --> 00:11:09,927
- [Crow] Don't need that.

297
00:11:09,927 --> 00:11:11,508
- [Tom] Hey!

298
00:11:11,508 --> 00:11:12,341
- Hold it!

299
00:11:14,016 --> 00:11:16,683
(monkey sounds)

300
00:11:18,419 --> 00:11:22,840
- [Crow] Ah, just save
the kid, stop showing off.

301
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:24,393
- [Tom] That's what happens when you spend

302
00:11:24,393 --> 00:11:26,976
a lot around sailors, you know?

303
00:11:27,977 --> 00:11:31,292
- [Joel] Thank God they built
this place like a jungle gym.

304
00:11:31,292 --> 00:11:33,245
- [Tom] Here, Billy, let
me close the window first.

305
00:11:33,245 --> 00:11:35,644
- Let go, Billy, I've got ya.

306
00:11:35,644 --> 00:11:37,727
- [Joel] Give us Baradas!

307
00:11:39,613 --> 00:11:42,530
- [Joel] Here, catch him, you mugs.

308
00:11:43,928 --> 00:11:46,925
- [Crow] Ah, you got acne
all over your back, yuck!

309
00:11:46,925 --> 00:11:48,580
- [Tom] Hey, kid, feel free to take

310
00:11:48,580 --> 00:11:51,359
your heel out of my groin.

311
00:11:51,359 --> 00:11:53,291
- [Joel] Now somebody's gotta save me.

312
00:11:53,291 --> 00:11:54,291
- [Tom] Doh!

313
00:11:56,709 --> 00:11:59,292
(violin music)

314
00:12:00,218 --> 00:12:01,977
- [Joel] Thanks, friend.

315
00:12:01,977 --> 00:12:03,627
- Hold tight, Billy.

316
00:12:03,627 --> 00:12:06,754
- [Crow] Augh, not that tight. (chokes)

317
00:12:06,754 --> 00:12:08,171
- [Tom] Yay, woo.

318
00:12:09,046 --> 00:12:09,879
Tada!

319
00:12:09,879 --> 00:12:10,965
(cheers)

320
00:12:10,965 --> 00:12:12,195
- I thought the kid was a goner.

321
00:12:12,195 --> 00:12:13,334
- What were you doing up there?

322
00:12:13,334 --> 00:12:15,850
- Dad sent me over to get you,
but they wouldn't let me in.

323
00:12:15,850 --> 00:12:17,036
- Has he had another earthquake warning?

324
00:12:17,036 --> 00:12:19,152
- Yeah, he wants you to come right over.

325
00:12:19,152 --> 00:12:20,539
- Wait until I get into my clothes.

326
00:12:20,539 --> 00:12:22,162
I'm going over to Professor
Norton's laboratory.

327
00:12:22,162 --> 00:12:23,104
- [Tom] Oh no, just put on a sarong.

328
00:12:23,104 --> 00:12:25,844
That'll be all you need.

329
00:12:25,844 --> 00:12:26,677
- [Woman] What you say

330
00:12:26,677 --> 00:12:27,735
is very interesting, Professor Norton.

331
00:12:27,735 --> 00:12:28,860
- [Crow] But stupid.

332
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:31,843
- This little machine
can predict earthquakes.

333
00:12:31,843 --> 00:12:33,808
- Not only predicts, but prevents them.

334
00:12:33,808 --> 00:12:36,141
- [Joel] And it makes toast.

335
00:12:37,210 --> 00:12:38,930
- Ah, there you are, Lieutenant.

336
00:12:38,930 --> 00:12:40,861
The signals are coming in much stronger.

337
00:12:40,861 --> 00:12:43,025
- [Joel] Oh, the earth just moved for me.

338
00:12:43,025 --> 00:12:45,930
- And this is Miss Compton,
staff writer of the Times.

339
00:12:45,930 --> 00:12:48,192
- Everybody knows Crash Corrigan.

340
00:12:48,192 --> 00:12:50,748
- [Tom] Everybody digs Dill Evans. So?

341
00:12:50,748 --> 00:12:53,298
- [Joel] Answer unclear, ask again.

342
00:12:53,298 --> 00:12:55,280
(electronic buzzing)

343
00:12:55,280 --> 00:12:56,113
- [Tom] Popcorn's ready.

344
00:12:56,113 --> 00:12:57,346
- [Crow] And it makes three
different kinds of gelati.

345
00:12:57,346 --> 00:12:58,896
- It's that signal again.

346
00:12:58,896 --> 00:13:01,169
They've been sending
it every five minutes.

347
00:13:01,169 --> 00:13:03,386
- They? Just what do you mean by they?

348
00:13:03,386 --> 00:13:04,219
- [Tom] Comedy central.

349
00:13:04,219 --> 00:13:06,026
- Professor Norton
maintains that these signals

350
00:13:06,026 --> 00:13:08,154
must be the work of some human agency,

351
00:13:08,154 --> 00:13:10,400
apparently coming from
the bottom of the ocean.

352
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:11,897
- [Joel] Oh, the William Wall sages.

353
00:13:11,897 --> 00:13:13,200
- I hope you're not
spinning that fantastic yarn

354
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:15,366
about the lost continent of Atlantis.

355
00:13:15,366 --> 00:13:19,202
- Exactly, only now I have
some definite evidence.

356
00:13:19,202 --> 00:13:21,215
- [Crow] Let me get in my
costume and tell you about it.

357
00:13:21,215 --> 00:13:23,117
Now pretend I'm a swarthy pirate.

358
00:13:23,117 --> 00:13:24,560
- This is pure orichalcum.

359
00:13:24,560 --> 00:13:25,393
- [Joel] No I'm not!

360
00:13:25,393 --> 00:13:27,625
- A metal made by fusing gold and copper.

361
00:13:27,625 --> 00:13:30,916
The secret of this process
was lost with the Atlantians.

362
00:13:30,916 --> 00:13:32,325
- What does that prove?

363
00:13:32,325 --> 00:13:33,616
- Well, according to every test,

364
00:13:33,616 --> 00:13:36,335
the idol couldn't be more
than a couple of years old,

365
00:13:36,335 --> 00:13:37,752
something made recently.

366
00:13:37,752 --> 00:13:39,233
I found it during a recent trip I made

367
00:13:39,233 --> 00:13:42,836
in my rocket submarine
in this general location.

368
00:13:42,836 --> 00:13:44,898
- [Crow] You have a rich
inner life, don't you?

369
00:13:44,898 --> 00:13:47,085
- The ancient continent
of Atlantis was reported

370
00:13:47,085 --> 00:13:49,264
to have sunk thousands of years ago.

371
00:13:49,264 --> 00:13:52,614
Contrary to popular belief,
Atlantis did not sink overnight,

372
00:13:52,614 --> 00:13:54,029
but during a period of years.

373
00:13:54,029 --> 00:13:54,997
- [Tom] That's great, Gramps.

374
00:13:54,997 --> 00:13:56,580
Get the medication!

375
00:13:57,629 --> 00:13:59,018
- To construct a roof of orichalcum

376
00:13:59,018 --> 00:14:00,872
over the city and keep out the ocean.

377
00:14:00,872 --> 00:14:02,313
- [Joel] Ah, film's melting!

378
00:14:02,313 --> 00:14:04,538
Oh, it's a better world.

379
00:14:04,538 --> 00:14:06,429
- [Tom] Xanadu, stately underwater home

380
00:14:06,429 --> 00:14:08,120
of Charles Foster Kane.

381
00:14:08,120 --> 00:14:09,953
Cost? No fish can say.

382
00:14:14,132 --> 00:14:17,390
- [Crow] It's a Denver Pyle statue.

383
00:14:17,390 --> 00:14:20,211
- [Guard] I beg of you to
take safety in the citadel.

384
00:14:20,211 --> 00:14:22,345
- Poseidon, guard of Atlantis,

385
00:14:22,345 --> 00:14:25,262
has never forsaken his
people in time of need.

386
00:14:25,262 --> 00:14:26,187
- [Tom] Jiffy Pop's ready.

387
00:14:26,187 --> 00:14:29,437
- I promise you, he will not do so now.

388
00:14:30,552 --> 00:14:32,052
- [Joel] Imperial.

389
00:14:33,496 --> 00:14:34,424
- Surely, we have broken the cover

390
00:14:34,424 --> 00:14:36,786
of this evil usurper,

391
00:14:36,786 --> 00:14:37,619
Unga Khan.

392
00:14:39,573 --> 00:14:41,064
- [Joel] Oh no, the king's
command is gonna make us

393
00:14:41,064 --> 00:14:45,301
pretend we were in The
Rockettes again, run!

394
00:14:45,301 --> 00:14:47,786
(dramatic music)

395
00:14:47,786 --> 00:14:50,667
It's the 1968 Chrysler Mariback.

396
00:14:50,667 --> 00:14:52,667
(siren)

397
00:14:53,967 --> 00:14:55,300
- [Crow] Fondue!

398
00:14:56,908 --> 00:14:58,421
- Our men are outnumbered.

399
00:14:58,421 --> 00:15:00,147
The city is about to fall.

400
00:15:00,147 --> 00:15:03,475
- [Tom] Yeah, Mayor Dinkins
just ain't cutting it, is he?

401
00:15:03,475 --> 00:15:06,225
(dramatic music)

402
00:15:07,518 --> 00:15:08,351
Say...

403
00:15:10,473 --> 00:15:14,640
♫ Me and my shadow
walking down the avenue ♫

404
00:15:17,770 --> 00:15:20,187
- [Joel] Meh, must be Monday.

405
00:15:21,357 --> 00:15:23,756
- [Crow] Hm, traffic lights must be out.

406
00:15:23,756 --> 00:15:25,464
- [Tom] Yup.

407
00:15:25,464 --> 00:15:27,882
You believe this crap?

408
00:15:27,882 --> 00:15:28,715
Unreal.

409
00:15:28,715 --> 00:15:31,437
- [Crow] Hey, look, dung beetles.

410
00:15:31,437 --> 00:15:33,687
(shouting)

411
00:15:34,602 --> 00:15:36,102
I don't want that.

412
00:15:38,485 --> 00:15:41,008
(thunk)

413
00:15:41,008 --> 00:15:42,591
- [Tom] He was my favorite.

414
00:15:42,591 --> 00:15:44,990
(explosion)

415
00:15:44,990 --> 00:15:48,233
- [Crow] Ken just exploded, sir.

416
00:15:48,233 --> 00:15:49,366
(explosion)

417
00:15:49,366 --> 00:15:52,588
- [Joel] They bomb horses, don't they?

418
00:15:52,588 --> 00:15:54,639
(reverse signal)

419
00:15:54,639 --> 00:15:56,385
- [Tom] He's backing up.

420
00:15:56,385 --> 00:15:58,972
- [Crow] There's a bomb
going into the air shaft.

421
00:15:58,972 --> 00:16:01,406
(explosion)

422
00:16:01,406 --> 00:16:05,724
- [Joel] This place looks
like a bomb just hit it... oh.

423
00:16:05,724 --> 00:16:09,443
(spaceship engine sounds)

424
00:16:09,443 --> 00:16:11,211
- [Crow] Jane, I'm home!

425
00:16:11,211 --> 00:16:13,144
- [Joel] David Crosby, no!

426
00:16:13,144 --> 00:16:14,524
(sniff)

427
00:16:14,524 --> 00:16:15,714
- Sharad's army has been driven

428
00:16:15,714 --> 00:16:18,105
within the walls of the sacred city.

429
00:16:18,105 --> 00:16:20,434
- Good, recall the troops.

430
00:16:20,434 --> 00:16:22,684
If those religious
fanatics donate control,

431
00:16:22,684 --> 00:16:24,050
I'll have no more interference

432
00:16:24,050 --> 00:16:26,816
with my plans to destroy the upper world.

433
00:16:26,816 --> 00:16:28,462
- [Crow] Heh, you are
really something, Boss.

434
00:16:28,462 --> 00:16:30,629
I think this is your year.

435
00:16:31,847 --> 00:16:34,299
- [Joel] Your hat's very
creative today, Kyle.

436
00:16:34,299 --> 00:16:37,467
- When you do succeed at selling
to the bottom of the sea,

437
00:16:37,467 --> 00:16:40,799
Atlantis will rise once more
to its former place in the sun,

438
00:16:40,799 --> 00:16:42,692
and you will be ruler of all things.

439
00:16:42,692 --> 00:16:44,380
- Start the Disintegrator!

440
00:16:44,380 --> 00:16:45,963
- [Joel] Duh, okay.

441
00:16:48,906 --> 00:16:51,285
- [Tom] Go finish your
giant Rolaid first, sir.

442
00:16:51,285 --> 00:16:52,719
(gags)

443
00:16:52,719 --> 00:16:55,070
Oh look, here's Daniel in the lion's den,

444
00:16:55,070 --> 00:16:56,897
this clown's making chains,

445
00:16:56,897 --> 00:16:58,991
this is a giant Welch's
grape jelly machine,

446
00:16:58,991 --> 00:16:59,824
and I'm out of here!

447
00:16:59,824 --> 00:17:00,858
Thank you very much, ladies and gentlemen.

448
00:17:00,858 --> 00:17:02,767
Goodnight now!

449
00:17:02,767 --> 00:17:06,938
- [Crow] It's the camera
crew from Real Life.

450
00:17:06,938 --> 00:17:08,456
(dramatic music)

451
00:17:08,456 --> 00:17:10,077
- [Joel] Set it for stun.

452
00:17:10,077 --> 00:17:12,739
- [Tom] Let's play some metal, woo!

453
00:17:12,739 --> 00:17:14,575
- [Crow] You clinking,
clanking, clattering

454
00:17:14,575 --> 00:17:16,708
collection of caliginous junk!

455
00:17:16,708 --> 00:17:19,848
- [Joel] Oh look, he's
packing a tummy massager.

456
00:17:19,848 --> 00:17:21,523
(crumbling)

457
00:17:21,523 --> 00:17:24,881
- [All] ♫ The night Chicago died

458
00:17:24,881 --> 00:17:26,602
♫ Chicago died

459
00:17:26,602 --> 00:17:29,547
♫ Brother, what a night the people saw ♫

460
00:17:29,547 --> 00:17:30,925
- [Newscaster] Red Cross
is beginning to document

461
00:17:30,925 --> 00:17:33,210
submissions leading to rushing
supplies into the area.

462
00:17:33,210 --> 00:17:34,043
- [Crow] You in there?

463
00:17:34,043 --> 00:17:35,198
- [Tom] Sold American.

464
00:17:35,198 --> 00:17:36,114
- [Newscaster] Martial
law is rushing a militia

465
00:17:36,114 --> 00:17:37,319
to the scenes of the disaster.

466
00:17:37,319 --> 00:17:39,683
- [Tom] Now on the lighter
side of the news...

467
00:17:39,683 --> 00:17:40,713
- Saint Clair?

468
00:17:40,713 --> 00:17:42,694
That's only 300 miles from here.

469
00:17:42,694 --> 00:17:44,431
- Yes, and according to my calculations,

470
00:17:44,431 --> 00:17:47,003
another severe shock
will occur any moment.

471
00:17:47,003 --> 00:17:47,866
- [Joel] Dang.

472
00:17:47,866 --> 00:17:50,179
- Put that contracting machine
in a submarine right away.

473
00:17:50,179 --> 00:17:51,012
- [Tom] Duh, okay.

474
00:17:51,012 --> 00:17:52,629
- You're going down to
try to stop this quake?

475
00:17:52,629 --> 00:17:54,913
What a story this will make for my paper!

476
00:17:54,913 --> 00:17:59,080
(Mystery Science Theater 3000 theme)

477
00:18:00,794 --> 00:18:04,817
- [Joel] Troy Tempest,
phones equal Stingray.

478
00:18:04,817 --> 00:18:07,848
- [Crow] Great lady down for cocktail.

479
00:18:07,848 --> 00:18:09,483
- Professor Norton.

480
00:18:09,483 --> 00:18:10,935
- [Tom] Norton, get in here.

481
00:18:10,935 --> 00:18:12,297
Oh, you're getting in here.

482
00:18:12,297 --> 00:18:13,130
- I don't know.

483
00:18:13,130 --> 00:18:14,850
We're on a level keel, but
we're being dragged down

484
00:18:14,850 --> 00:18:16,317
by some mysterious force.

485
00:18:16,317 --> 00:18:17,797
- [Joel] The plot?

486
00:18:17,797 --> 00:18:19,771
(motor running)

487
00:18:19,771 --> 00:18:21,471
- [Crow] We're out of baking soda, sir.

488
00:18:21,471 --> 00:18:25,303
- [Joel] This sub is powered by Fizzies.

489
00:18:25,303 --> 00:18:28,349
(bubble noises)

490
00:18:28,349 --> 00:18:32,091
- [Crow] I want to take
us down to 20,000 leagues.

491
00:18:32,091 --> 00:18:34,349
- [Tom] Meanwhile, in
young Billy's bathtub.

492
00:18:34,349 --> 00:18:36,441
- [Crow] Billy Bathtub,
what a terrible movie.

493
00:18:36,441 --> 00:18:38,186
- [Tom] No, no, Crow.

494
00:18:38,186 --> 00:18:40,403
- [Joel] Check it out,
there are fish out there.

495
00:18:40,403 --> 00:18:41,486
- [Crow] Sea.

496
00:18:42,720 --> 00:18:46,851
- [Tom] They got a Genie automatic
garage door picker-upper.

497
00:18:46,851 --> 00:18:48,267
- [Joel] That's a Swiss Army joke.

498
00:18:48,267 --> 00:18:49,434
- [Tom] Sorry.

499
00:18:51,441 --> 00:18:53,284
- They're in the tunnel.

500
00:18:53,284 --> 00:18:55,735
- Bring them up through the Inland Sea.

501
00:18:55,735 --> 00:18:59,485
- [Crow] Do you think
I'm losing weight, sir?

502
00:19:00,712 --> 00:19:02,276
- [Joel] When in Coral Gables, Florida,

503
00:19:02,276 --> 00:19:04,443
visit Crash Corrigan Cove.

504
00:19:06,474 --> 00:19:07,964
- We're on the surface, I can see land.

505
00:19:07,964 --> 00:19:08,797
- [Tom] Captain Billy.

506
00:19:08,797 --> 00:19:09,630
- Impossible.

507
00:19:09,630 --> 00:19:10,786
According to the depth indicator,

508
00:19:10,786 --> 00:19:12,678
we're still some 10,000 feet down.

509
00:19:12,678 --> 00:19:14,758
- [Tom] I think they give
that kid too much freedom.

510
00:19:14,758 --> 00:19:15,976
- [Crash] That's land, alright.

511
00:19:15,976 --> 00:19:17,105
We're moving into a small inlet.

512
00:19:17,105 --> 00:19:21,109
- [Joel] Yup, that's land, I've
seen it before, definitely.

513
00:19:21,109 --> 00:19:22,078
- [Crow] Wait a minute.

514
00:19:22,078 --> 00:19:26,325
I think we're on the Pirates
of the Caribbean ride.

515
00:19:26,325 --> 00:19:27,158
- [Tom] Big sub.

516
00:19:27,158 --> 00:19:29,491
- Gee, we're on the surface.

517
00:19:32,723 --> 00:19:34,886
- [Tom] I knew Colonel Kurtz was close.

518
00:19:34,886 --> 00:19:36,647
He's real close.

519
00:19:36,647 --> 00:19:38,730
- [Crow] He knows Scotty.

520
00:19:41,017 --> 00:19:43,850
(bumbling noises)

521
00:19:46,542 --> 00:19:48,232
- Call out the imperial guard.

522
00:19:48,232 --> 00:19:50,566
- [Crow] And kill the dorky
guy, I hate comic relief.

523
00:19:50,566 --> 00:19:51,431
(beeping)

524
00:19:51,431 --> 00:19:52,264
- [Tom] That you're beeper?

525
00:19:52,264 --> 00:19:53,097
- [Crow] Nope.

526
00:19:53,097 --> 00:19:54,055
- [Tom] Well it's not me, I don't think.

527
00:19:54,055 --> 00:19:55,895
- Imperial to stable.

528
00:19:55,895 --> 00:19:58,469
- I command that his
supreme majesty Unga Khan,

529
00:19:58,469 --> 00:20:00,164
the detachment of the imperial guard

530
00:20:00,164 --> 00:20:02,019
will proceed at once to the Inland Sea

531
00:20:02,019 --> 00:20:04,492
and capture the stranger
from the upper world.

532
00:20:04,492 --> 00:20:06,209
- [Crow] Put your skirt down.

533
00:20:06,209 --> 00:20:07,729
- Aboard, quickly!

534
00:20:07,729 --> 00:20:09,481
Number one patrol, huh?

535
00:20:09,481 --> 00:20:11,494
- Number one patrol, prepare to move out!

536
00:20:11,494 --> 00:20:12,394
- [Joel] That's us, boys, come on.

537
00:20:12,394 --> 00:20:13,594
It's time for the big Hawaiian number.

538
00:20:13,594 --> 00:20:15,594
Hey, come on, everybody.

539
00:20:17,303 --> 00:20:19,423
- [Crow] Hey, it's the
Ponderosa under the sea.

540
00:20:19,423 --> 00:20:24,320
- [Joel] You know what, I
think that's a Mac page.

541
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:27,797
- [Tom] We now join Fitzcarraldo,
already in progress.

542
00:20:27,797 --> 00:20:29,237
(war cries)

543
00:20:29,237 --> 00:20:31,137
They running over somebody?

544
00:20:31,137 --> 00:20:32,841
- [Crow] Yee-hah!

545
00:20:32,841 --> 00:20:33,974
- [Joel] This looks like a fine place

546
00:20:33,974 --> 00:20:35,907
to set down my pasty white bottom.

547
00:20:35,907 --> 00:20:38,621
- [Crow] Well, they're certainly
well dressed for adventure.

548
00:20:38,621 --> 00:20:41,464
- Here we are, 10,000
feet below sea level.

549
00:20:41,464 --> 00:20:42,343
- [Tom] And me without my rubbers.

550
00:20:42,343 --> 00:20:45,427
- Apparently in another world.

551
00:20:45,427 --> 00:20:47,502
- [Crow] Hey, he's being
followed by Michael Nesmith.

552
00:20:47,502 --> 00:20:51,058
- [Joel] Hey, did you know
my mom invented liquid paper?

553
00:20:51,058 --> 00:20:52,784
(parrot cawing)

554
00:20:52,784 --> 00:20:54,179
- [Tom] Toki, toki!

555
00:20:54,179 --> 00:20:56,341
- Submerge the submarine while
we investigate this place.

556
00:20:56,341 --> 00:20:58,357
- [Tom] Alright.

557
00:20:58,357 --> 00:21:01,420
(foreboding music)

558
00:21:01,420 --> 00:21:05,626
- [Joel] Hey, did you
remember to close the hatch?

559
00:21:05,626 --> 00:21:07,422
- [Crow] Well, remember
where we parked, everyone.

560
00:21:07,422 --> 00:21:08,489
We're in the giraffe lot.

561
00:21:08,489 --> 00:21:09,840
- We have to hide the control box.

562
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:11,064
- Now there's a good place.

563
00:21:11,064 --> 00:21:11,897
- Yes, that'll do.

564
00:21:11,897 --> 00:21:14,190
- You think we better
wait for Briny and Salty?

565
00:21:14,190 --> 00:21:16,504
- [Tom] Briny and Salty,
the Pretzel twins.

566
00:21:16,504 --> 00:21:18,418
- Do you want us to catch the parrot?

567
00:21:18,418 --> 00:21:19,986
(galloping)

568
00:21:19,986 --> 00:21:20,819
- [Crow] Hey, everybody!

569
00:21:20,819 --> 00:21:24,222
Here's where the two roads
diverge into a yellow wood.

570
00:21:24,222 --> 00:21:27,139
- [Tom] Take the one less traveled.

571
00:21:28,275 --> 00:21:30,051
- [Joel] That is one sweet chariot.

572
00:21:30,051 --> 00:21:32,617
- [Crow] Shirley Maclaine.

573
00:21:32,617 --> 00:21:34,501
- [Tom] No, we are lost, R2.

574
00:21:34,501 --> 00:21:37,039
(whistles)

575
00:21:37,039 --> 00:21:40,172
- [All] Owie, ouch,
ouch, ouch, stingy, ouch.

576
00:21:40,172 --> 00:21:41,005
- [Tom] Shouldn't they be riding

577
00:21:41,005 --> 00:21:42,677
side-saddle in those skirts?

578
00:21:42,677 --> 00:21:43,969
- [Crow] Some of them are.

579
00:21:43,969 --> 00:21:46,322
(galloping)

580
00:21:46,322 --> 00:21:47,449
(whistles)

581
00:21:47,449 --> 00:21:48,579
- [Crow] No, you go that way.

582
00:21:48,579 --> 00:21:50,349
You'll be breaking down in no time.

583
00:21:50,349 --> 00:21:53,178
(whistles)

584
00:21:53,178 --> 00:21:55,678
- [Joel] Hey, it's the 34
horsemen of the apocalypse.

585
00:21:55,678 --> 00:21:59,179
Ouch, stingy, ouch, that burns, ouch.

586
00:21:59,179 --> 00:22:02,222
Hey, check it out, it says
Bill Shatner was here.

587
00:22:02,222 --> 00:22:06,708
- [Tom] Great, they're letting
Jay North lead the pack here.

588
00:22:06,708 --> 00:22:07,762
(suspenseful music)

589
00:22:07,762 --> 00:22:08,595
- Look, Dad!

590
00:22:08,595 --> 00:22:10,222
- [Crow] How come they all
turned when he said Dad?

591
00:22:10,222 --> 00:22:11,790
- Unless my calculations are wrong,

592
00:22:11,790 --> 00:22:14,037
we've come across the lost
continent of Atlantis.

593
00:22:14,037 --> 00:22:17,010
- [Tom] I'd say your
calculations are wrong.

594
00:22:17,010 --> 00:22:18,429
- [Diane] It must be a mirage.

595
00:22:18,429 --> 00:22:20,108
- [Crow] No, the Mirage is on the Strip.

596
00:22:20,108 --> 00:22:21,360
- What is that?

597
00:22:21,360 --> 00:22:22,493
(galloping)

598
00:22:22,493 --> 00:22:23,689
- [Joel] And around the clubhouse turn

599
00:22:23,689 --> 00:22:25,189
is Apartment House With Plenty of Room

600
00:22:25,189 --> 00:22:29,744
with Bird Droppings on the
Rail coming around the bend.

601
00:22:29,744 --> 00:22:31,491
- [Tom] Oh, look, this is what
starts those chain accidents.

602
00:22:31,491 --> 00:22:32,622
- That's no illusion.

603
00:22:32,622 --> 00:22:35,455
- [Joel] It's a world of illusion.

604
00:22:36,469 --> 00:22:37,886
- Look, soldiers!

605
00:22:39,286 --> 00:22:40,557
(galloping)

606
00:22:40,557 --> 00:22:43,269
- [Crow] Wait there, we're
coming to attack you.

607
00:22:43,269 --> 00:22:45,207
- Keep out of sight.

608
00:22:45,207 --> 00:22:46,924
Until we find out if
they're friends or enemies.

609
00:22:46,924 --> 00:22:51,085
- [Tom] If they kill me,
they're probably enemies.

610
00:22:51,085 --> 00:22:53,958
(suspenseful music)

611
00:22:53,958 --> 00:22:57,249
- [Joel] Uh, taxi, I say, taxi!

612
00:22:57,249 --> 00:22:59,832
(music swells)

613
00:23:01,201 --> 00:23:02,872
- Capture him!

614
00:23:02,872 --> 00:23:05,812
- [Crow] Gah! He's a real diplomat.

615
00:23:05,812 --> 00:23:08,888
(dramatic music)

616
00:23:08,888 --> 00:23:12,196
- [Joel] Get him, girls, he hit Buddy!

617
00:23:12,196 --> 00:23:14,529
(straining)

618
00:23:17,741 --> 00:23:20,582
- [Tom] Hey, get this, I can
see up Trent's skirt from here.

619
00:23:20,582 --> 00:23:23,763
- [Crow] Okay, hand me up that horse.

620
00:23:23,763 --> 00:23:26,513
(dramatic music)

621
00:23:28,006 --> 00:23:29,962
- [Tom] Uh-oh.

622
00:23:29,962 --> 00:23:33,331
I got a crazy idea,
but it just might work.

623
00:23:33,331 --> 00:23:34,364
- [Joel] No way they're gonna pants me

624
00:23:34,364 --> 00:23:36,364
and make me one of them!

625
00:23:37,873 --> 00:23:40,678
(galloping)

626
00:23:40,678 --> 00:23:43,009
- [Tom] Well I never rid a horsey before.

627
00:23:43,009 --> 00:23:44,296
- He's escaping.

628
00:23:44,296 --> 00:23:45,547
- Head out to check him out.

629
00:23:45,547 --> 00:23:48,184
- [Tom] Enough said, live to surf.

630
00:23:48,184 --> 00:23:51,184
(slow circus music)

631
00:23:53,730 --> 00:23:56,063
(galloping)

632
00:23:57,166 --> 00:23:59,370
- [Crow] Wait, we need that horse.

633
00:23:59,370 --> 00:24:01,694
- You sure fooled them this time, Crash.

634
00:24:01,694 --> 00:24:02,844
- Let's get back to the submarine.

635
00:24:02,844 --> 00:24:04,111
- But the villagers, what about them?

636
00:24:04,111 --> 00:24:05,608
- I'll tell you later,
there's no time to lose.

637
00:24:05,608 --> 00:24:06,441
- Hurry, Diana!

638
00:24:06,441 --> 00:24:07,376
- [All] Hurry, Diana!

639
00:24:07,376 --> 00:24:08,472
- [Joel] Well around about that time,

640
00:24:08,472 --> 00:24:12,848
the Duke boys did a little body
work on the old General Lee.

641
00:24:12,848 --> 00:24:16,334
- [Tom] ♫ Hot blooded, check it and see ♫

642
00:24:16,334 --> 00:24:20,001
- [All] (sing Batman theme)

643
00:24:22,164 --> 00:24:23,496
- Some sort of a war tank.

644
00:24:23,496 --> 00:24:24,329
- [All] Oh, I see...

645
00:24:24,329 --> 00:24:25,329
A war tank?!

646
00:24:27,404 --> 00:24:31,386
- [Joel] Excuse me, have
you seen any Earthlings?

647
00:24:31,386 --> 00:24:32,219
- Steady.

648
00:24:32,219 --> 00:24:34,243
- [Tom] Hold me!

649
00:24:34,243 --> 00:24:37,661
- [Crow] Okay, boys, delouse them.

650
00:24:37,661 --> 00:24:38,804
(gunshot)

651
00:24:38,804 --> 00:24:40,867
- [Tom] Oh, right in the nostril.

652
00:24:40,867 --> 00:24:43,468
(gunshot)

653
00:24:43,468 --> 00:24:45,056
- [Joel] Fissure, fissure.

654
00:24:45,056 --> 00:24:46,271
- They're not human!

655
00:24:46,271 --> 00:24:48,057
- [Tom] Uh, yeah, we noticed.

656
00:24:48,057 --> 00:24:50,721
Stop, wait, come back.

657
00:24:50,721 --> 00:24:52,602
(explosion)

658
00:24:52,602 --> 00:24:54,967
(robotic sounds)

659
00:24:54,967 --> 00:24:56,967
(splat)

660
00:24:58,067 --> 00:25:02,234
- [All] ♫ I'm a little
teapot, short and stout

661
00:25:03,291 --> 00:25:05,988
♫ This is my handle, this is my gun

662
00:25:05,988 --> 00:25:09,423
♫ One is for pouring, one is for fun ♫

663
00:25:09,423 --> 00:25:11,477
- [Tom] (laughing) Pretty good.

664
00:25:11,477 --> 00:25:12,806
(explosion)

665
00:25:12,806 --> 00:25:13,889
- [Joel] Doh!

666
00:25:15,688 --> 00:25:17,071
(explosion)

667
00:25:17,071 --> 00:25:18,238
- [Tom] Moses.

668
00:25:19,892 --> 00:25:20,858
Silly string!

669
00:25:20,858 --> 00:25:22,378
(screaming)

670
00:25:22,378 --> 00:25:24,711
- [Crow] Geez, lady, scream
in someone else's ear.

671
00:25:24,711 --> 00:25:25,544
- [Tom] Really.

672
00:25:25,544 --> 00:25:27,397
- You two get behind that rock.

673
00:25:27,397 --> 00:25:29,599
- [Joel] Count up by ones, everyone.

674
00:25:29,599 --> 00:25:30,766
- [All] One...

675
00:25:32,890 --> 00:25:34,298
- [Tom] Here, Billy, you draw there fire.

676
00:25:34,298 --> 00:25:35,234
- [Joel] Diane!

677
00:25:35,234 --> 00:25:36,505
- The strangers have escaped...

678
00:25:36,505 --> 00:25:40,496
- [Crow] I hate when they
talk during the movie... oh.

679
00:25:40,496 --> 00:25:43,246
(dramatic music)

680
00:25:46,219 --> 00:25:49,610
- Alas, the invisible ray
gun will soon stop them.

681
00:25:49,610 --> 00:25:51,010
(static)

682
00:25:51,010 --> 00:25:52,315
- [Joel] That's not very invisible.

683
00:25:52,315 --> 00:25:54,065
- [Tom] I can see it.

684
00:25:54,972 --> 00:25:56,459
(dramatic music)

685
00:25:56,459 --> 00:25:57,743
- [Crow] Alright, Billy,
when I throw you off here,

686
00:25:57,743 --> 00:25:59,411
flap your arms like crazy.

687
00:25:59,411 --> 00:26:01,578
- Start the Disintegrator!

688
00:26:02,634 --> 00:26:06,184
- [Joel] GE, we bring
good things to death.

689
00:26:06,184 --> 00:26:08,351
(zapping)

690
00:26:09,333 --> 00:26:11,583
(rumbling)

691
00:26:12,561 --> 00:26:16,083
- [Tom] You know, I like Meg
Ryan in this role better.

692
00:26:16,083 --> 00:26:17,218
(rumbling)

693
00:26:17,218 --> 00:26:18,212
- [Joel] Well, this is it.

694
00:26:18,212 --> 00:26:20,954
Looks like I'm gonna be
forced to push you off, Billy.

695
00:26:20,954 --> 00:26:23,820
- [Tom] Hit them in the mouth?

696
00:26:23,820 --> 00:26:25,503
Oh no, they're shooting hot dogs at them.

697
00:26:25,503 --> 00:26:29,294
- [Crow] (laughs) Hold
the mustard on that one.

698
00:26:29,294 --> 00:26:31,859
- [Joel] Admiral, deploy
the cheddar wurst.

699
00:26:31,859 --> 00:26:33,218
- [Tom] Oh no!

700
00:26:33,218 --> 00:26:34,051
- Boom.

701
00:26:35,683 --> 00:26:37,964
(explosion)

702
00:26:37,964 --> 00:26:40,214
(rumbling)

703
00:26:47,870 --> 00:26:50,532
- Oh, wow, blindsided by another short.

704
00:26:50,532 --> 00:26:52,032
Are you guys okay?

705
00:26:52,032 --> 00:26:52,997
- Ah, don't worry, Joel.

706
00:26:52,997 --> 00:26:54,697
I'm feeling no unusual effects from it.

707
00:26:54,697 --> 00:26:56,173
Isn't that right, Billy?

708
00:26:56,173 --> 00:26:57,892
That's right, Crow.

709
00:26:57,892 --> 00:27:00,430
- That's really nice, Crow.

710
00:27:00,430 --> 00:27:02,587
You know, Joel, for the life of me,

711
00:27:02,587 --> 00:27:05,405
I can't understand the
appeal of these old serials.

712
00:27:05,405 --> 00:27:07,795
- Well that's no big
mystery, my little fire plug.

713
00:27:07,795 --> 00:27:10,922
You see, they actually have
kind of a historic quality.

714
00:27:10,922 --> 00:27:12,517
If it's in the 1930s,

715
00:27:12,517 --> 00:27:14,800
and you're interested in
taking over the world,

716
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:17,136
you gotta get gussied up to beat the man.

717
00:27:17,136 --> 00:27:18,653
- No kidding, just look at the

718
00:27:18,653 --> 00:27:20,335
elaborately flamboyant costumes

719
00:27:20,335 --> 00:27:22,289
the bad guys were gadding about in.

720
00:27:22,289 --> 00:27:26,456
Togas, electric helmets,
dance belts, fascist underoos.

721
00:27:27,554 --> 00:27:29,476
- Exactly, and I thought it'd be fun,

722
00:27:29,476 --> 00:27:32,032
as a thought experiment, for you guys

723
00:27:32,032 --> 00:27:33,589
to tell me how you would dress up

724
00:27:33,589 --> 00:27:35,779
if you were gonna take over the world.

725
00:27:35,779 --> 00:27:37,478
- Oh, you know, I'd bronze my skin,

726
00:27:37,478 --> 00:27:40,485
put on 40 pounds, and
dress like Bea Arthur.

727
00:27:40,485 --> 00:27:41,535
- I'd wear a Lone Ranger mask

728
00:27:41,535 --> 00:27:44,086
and a swimsuit made of string cheese.

729
00:27:44,086 --> 00:27:45,285
- Good idea, very thoughtful.

730
00:27:45,285 --> 00:27:48,435
Okay, say you've got your costume.

731
00:27:48,435 --> 00:27:49,850
What would your caper be?

732
00:27:49,850 --> 00:27:52,227
I mean, what would you do
to take over the world?

733
00:27:52,227 --> 00:27:53,948
- I'd freeze all the world's oceans.

734
00:27:53,948 --> 00:27:55,389
But then, I guess that means

735
00:27:55,389 --> 00:27:57,404
I'd have to dress like Dick Button.

736
00:27:57,404 --> 00:27:59,752
- I'd send California
tumbling into the ocean.

737
00:27:59,752 --> 00:28:00,966
The Atlantic Ocean.

738
00:28:00,966 --> 00:28:02,833
After showering, tauk, and changing

739
00:28:02,833 --> 00:28:04,981
into my Frito Bandito costume.

740
00:28:04,981 --> 00:28:08,263
- I see, well once you've
taken over the world,

741
00:28:08,263 --> 00:28:09,714
what would you do then?

742
00:28:09,714 --> 00:28:11,495
- Oh! I'd turn all those little Pose Me

743
00:28:11,495 --> 00:28:14,655
into the boy in the plastic
bubble for obvious reasons.

744
00:28:14,655 --> 00:28:17,072
- I'd declare March, Liza Month!

745
00:28:17,072 --> 00:28:17,905
- Woo!

746
00:28:17,905 --> 00:28:19,796
- Well, it's fun to dream, you know.

747
00:28:19,796 --> 00:28:22,905
But you can bet that
anybody seriously interested

748
00:28:22,905 --> 00:28:25,002
in world domination is gonna end up

749
00:28:25,002 --> 00:28:27,163
looking like a real Snickerdoodle.

750
00:28:27,163 --> 00:28:28,703
- What's that supposed to mean?

751
00:28:28,703 --> 00:28:30,315
I'll deal with you later.

752
00:28:30,315 --> 00:28:32,012
Back to the drill, Frank.

753
00:28:32,012 --> 00:28:34,436
You will bow down before
me, Son of Jor-El.

754
00:28:34,436 --> 00:28:36,016
Bow down!

755
00:28:36,016 --> 00:28:40,183
(Mystery Science Theater 3000 theme)

756
00:28:41,632 --> 00:28:44,382
(dramatic music)

757
00:28:49,437 --> 00:28:52,522
- [Crow] Looks like an E.
E. Cummings book cover.

758
00:28:52,522 --> 00:28:55,022
(eerie music)

759
00:28:57,640 --> 00:29:00,807
- [Joel] Type setting by Smith Corona.

760
00:29:06,517 --> 00:29:07,764
- [Tom] Hoo!

761
00:29:07,764 --> 00:29:09,469
(Joel laughs)

762
00:29:09,469 --> 00:29:10,302
Neat, huh?

763
00:29:11,276 --> 00:29:14,392
- [Crow] I'll have a Dick
Rubin, hold the sauerkraut.

764
00:29:14,392 --> 00:29:17,225
- [Joel] Ryder Ren Sound Services.

765
00:29:20,193 --> 00:29:22,495
- [Tom] Laszlo also brings
you a fine line of cosmetics

766
00:29:22,495 --> 00:29:26,364
for aging socialites, and social x-rays.

767
00:29:26,364 --> 00:29:29,732
- [All] Hey, Roger Corman! Woo!

768
00:29:29,732 --> 00:29:32,130
- [Joel] Huh, I wonder if they're related.

769
00:29:32,130 --> 00:29:33,756
- [Tom] Oh, no.

770
00:29:33,756 --> 00:29:37,120
(dramatic music)

771
00:29:37,120 --> 00:29:39,482
- [Crow] Hmm, a title
sequence named Desire.

772
00:29:39,482 --> 00:29:40,732
- [Tom] Stella!

773
00:29:44,323 --> 00:29:45,659
- [Joel] Then folks started paying

774
00:29:45,659 --> 00:29:47,369
for their groceries with old cars.

775
00:29:47,369 --> 00:29:49,012
Nothing you could do about it.

776
00:29:49,012 --> 00:29:50,543
- [Tom] Nope.

777
00:29:50,543 --> 00:29:51,629
- [Crow] This looks like the right side

778
00:29:51,629 --> 00:29:54,298
of the wrong side of the tracks.

779
00:29:54,298 --> 00:29:55,546
(bell ringing)

780
00:29:55,546 --> 00:29:56,962
- [All] Norm!

781
00:29:56,962 --> 00:29:58,499
(laughter)

782
00:29:58,499 --> 00:29:59,895
- Now wait a minute, wait a minute.

783
00:29:59,895 --> 00:30:01,548
- [Tom] That was a Hee
Haw writing session.

784
00:30:01,548 --> 00:30:02,381
- [Crow] So I was thinking Lulu

785
00:30:02,381 --> 00:30:03,766
could pop up behind some corn.

786
00:30:03,766 --> 00:30:06,439
- [Joel] Now that's funny.

787
00:30:06,439 --> 00:30:07,272
- Liz!

788
00:30:07,272 --> 00:30:09,386
- [Joel] Is more.

789
00:30:09,386 --> 00:30:10,753
- You hear me, girl?

790
00:30:10,753 --> 00:30:13,582
- [Tom] Tell old Jerry Lee
to hold it down in there.

791
00:30:13,582 --> 00:30:15,718
(footsteps)

792
00:30:15,718 --> 00:30:19,022
- [Crow] Aye, who's the new girl?

793
00:30:19,022 --> 00:30:20,251
- [Tom] Lolita!

794
00:30:20,251 --> 00:30:22,729
- What do you want now?

795
00:30:22,729 --> 00:30:23,903
- [Crow] Are you thinking
what I'm thinking?

796
00:30:23,903 --> 00:30:25,570
- [Tom] Yeah, Crest!

797
00:30:26,453 --> 00:30:28,834
- You gotta play that thing so loud?

798
00:30:28,834 --> 00:30:32,096
What'd you come out like this for?

799
00:30:32,096 --> 00:30:32,929
- [Tom] Oral?

800
00:30:32,929 --> 00:30:34,719
- Don't bother me.

801
00:30:34,719 --> 00:30:35,552
- Liz.

802
00:30:35,552 --> 00:30:38,967
- [Crow] I wonder if they
have back porch on that place.

803
00:30:38,967 --> 00:30:40,762
- Some day, I'm gonna give that she-cat

804
00:30:40,762 --> 00:30:42,533
the whopping she's been asking for.

805
00:30:42,533 --> 00:30:43,533
- [All] Boo!

806
00:30:45,859 --> 00:30:47,506
- Let them know who wears
the pants around here.

807
00:30:47,506 --> 00:30:50,251
- [Joel] Yeah, and who wears
the stupid-looking hat.

808
00:30:50,251 --> 00:30:51,614
- Be back in a minute.

809
00:30:51,614 --> 00:30:54,346
- [Crow] Heh, good one, Ed,
I gotta give you that one.

810
00:30:54,346 --> 00:30:57,223
- Come back there, I'll lend you a jug.

811
00:30:57,223 --> 00:30:58,641
She'll be doing it.

812
00:30:58,641 --> 00:31:00,011
(laughs)

813
00:31:00,011 --> 00:31:01,224
- [Joel] Ha ha, I soiled myself.

814
00:31:01,224 --> 00:31:02,924
- Tell us about that thing you killed

815
00:31:02,924 --> 00:31:04,397
out there at the swamp, will ya?

816
00:31:04,397 --> 00:31:05,941
(indistinguishable)

817
00:31:05,941 --> 00:31:07,573
- [Tom] Maybe we should
have subtitles for this.

818
00:31:07,573 --> 00:31:11,445
- I put five slugs into that
critter before it went under.

819
00:31:11,445 --> 00:31:13,138
- [Crow] Then I had a few slugs myself.

820
00:31:13,138 --> 00:31:14,863
And then I went under.

821
00:31:14,863 --> 00:31:17,017
- Had regular arms on it like a man.

822
00:31:17,017 --> 00:31:18,272
- [Tom] But made for a woman.

823
00:31:18,272 --> 00:31:21,482
- Sorta different looking,
had suckers on them.

824
00:31:21,482 --> 00:31:23,049
- [Joel] Tootsie Pop, I think.

825
00:31:23,049 --> 00:31:25,266
- Like one of them...

826
00:31:25,266 --> 00:31:26,867
Like one of them octopuses.

827
00:31:26,867 --> 00:31:28,832
- [Crow] That's octopi, actually.

828
00:31:28,832 --> 00:31:31,433
- Boy, it was plum awful-looking.

829
00:31:31,433 --> 00:31:33,742
- Man, you sure that critter wasn't pink?

830
00:31:33,742 --> 00:31:34,687
(laughs)

831
00:31:34,687 --> 00:31:35,917
- [Crow] Shut up, Bruce.

832
00:31:35,917 --> 00:31:39,038
- I told you before, it
was sorta gray-looking.

833
00:31:39,038 --> 00:31:41,314
- [Tom] You gotta check
with String Bean to laugh?

834
00:31:41,314 --> 00:31:42,751
- Well, laugh if you want.

835
00:31:42,751 --> 00:31:43,584
- [Joel] Ha.

836
00:31:43,584 --> 00:31:46,387
- But that thing weren't
nothing nature put out there.

837
00:31:46,387 --> 00:31:49,933
No sir, and it weren't
no freak gator, neither.

838
00:31:49,933 --> 00:31:51,750
- [Crow] Maybe it was the rocket
fuel you've been drinking.

839
00:31:51,750 --> 00:31:53,112
- I've been poaching in
this country for 40 years

840
00:31:53,112 --> 00:31:55,955
and I ain't never seen nothing like it.

841
00:31:55,955 --> 00:31:57,590
- [Joel] Uh, that's a double negative.

842
00:31:57,590 --> 00:31:58,704
- Too bad you didn't bring it back.

843
00:31:58,704 --> 00:32:00,762
Steve Benton'd probably
pay a fair bounty for it,

844
00:32:00,762 --> 00:32:02,615
whatever it was.

845
00:32:02,615 --> 00:32:04,010
- Bounty?

846
00:32:04,010 --> 00:32:07,998
I wouldn't touch that critter
for all the money ever made.

847
00:32:07,998 --> 00:32:08,831
- [Tom] 10 bucks?

848
00:32:08,831 --> 00:32:11,573
- And if you'd seen it,
you wouldn't, neither.

849
00:32:11,573 --> 00:32:12,976
- You better take it easy on the stuff.

850
00:32:12,976 --> 00:32:14,372
You looking kinda taken.

851
00:32:14,372 --> 00:32:16,048
- [Joel] Oh, this is an intervention.

852
00:32:16,048 --> 00:32:17,909
- [Tom] Shucks, it's just tobacco juice.

853
00:32:17,909 --> 00:32:20,470
- I'm gonna clean my otter
lines out before morning.

854
00:32:20,470 --> 00:32:21,843
- [Crow] No! Not in front of us, please!

855
00:32:21,843 --> 00:32:24,750
- I think that critter's a
ghost of one of those otters

856
00:32:24,750 --> 00:32:26,160
you've been poaching all these years,

857
00:32:26,160 --> 00:32:27,856
coming back to haunt you.

858
00:32:27,856 --> 00:32:29,029
(laughs)

859
00:32:29,029 --> 00:32:30,591
- [Joel] Our joy, Crow's friend.

860
00:32:30,591 --> 00:32:33,646
- Well if that's so, you fellers

861
00:32:33,646 --> 00:32:37,164
will see it right quick yourselves.

862
00:32:37,164 --> 00:32:37,997
- [Tom] Yeah, that's great.

863
00:32:37,997 --> 00:32:41,361
When you get near a sentence, let us know.

864
00:32:41,361 --> 00:32:43,179
(yawn)

865
00:32:43,179 --> 00:32:44,012
- [Crow] Do you really think

866
00:32:44,012 --> 00:32:46,687
this loitering is good for business?

867
00:32:46,687 --> 00:32:48,538
(grunts)

868
00:32:48,538 --> 00:32:52,098
- [Joel] This sitting
around plum wore me out.

869
00:32:52,098 --> 00:32:54,681
- [Crow] The Hooterville Seven.

870
00:32:55,565 --> 00:32:59,463
- [Tom] I think she knows
how to scratch an itch.

871
00:32:59,463 --> 00:33:02,330
- [Joel] Ordinary pantyhose
are okay under funky clothes,

872
00:33:02,330 --> 00:33:04,843
but gentlemen prefer Hanes.

873
00:33:04,843 --> 00:33:06,673
- [Tom] Pete Fountain.

874
00:33:06,673 --> 00:33:09,729
(distant laughter)

875
00:33:09,729 --> 00:33:12,479
- [Crow] Quit wearing my things.

876
00:33:14,166 --> 00:33:16,083
- [Joel] Boss Hog, after dark.

877
00:33:16,083 --> 00:33:18,283
- Look, it's not like I wanna be mean

878
00:33:18,283 --> 00:33:20,629
or anything like that, Liz, baby.

879
00:33:20,629 --> 00:33:22,383
But I got a reputation around here.

880
00:33:22,383 --> 00:33:24,035
- [Crow] As a big fat insensitive guy?

881
00:33:24,035 --> 00:33:26,683
- No respect for a man who
lets his woman boss him.

882
00:33:26,683 --> 00:33:28,556
Next thing you know,
they'll be laughing at me.

883
00:33:28,556 --> 00:33:31,220
- [Tom] Then I'd have to
go on a shooting spree.

884
00:33:31,220 --> 00:33:35,136
- Won't you please
listen to me, Liz, baby?

885
00:33:35,136 --> 00:33:36,137
Liz?

886
00:33:36,137 --> 00:33:39,191
- [Joel] I'm not hearing you, la la la...

887
00:33:39,191 --> 00:33:40,855
♫ Hot child in the city ♫

888
00:33:40,855 --> 00:33:44,417
- [Tom] Sir, my name's
not Liz, it's Blanch.

889
00:33:44,417 --> 00:33:47,250
(seductive music)

890
00:33:49,864 --> 00:33:51,369
I love it when she puts it on there.

891
00:33:51,369 --> 00:33:53,202
I just like the smell.

892
00:33:54,825 --> 00:33:59,282
- [Crow] Liz, honey, you think
you could lose some weight?

893
00:33:59,282 --> 00:34:00,762
- [Joel] You know, she's gonna sharpen

894
00:34:00,762 --> 00:34:03,156
those calf muscles with those heels.

895
00:34:03,156 --> 00:34:05,989
(seductive music)

896
00:34:08,162 --> 00:34:10,688
- Stop looking at me like that.

897
00:34:10,688 --> 00:34:13,215
- I'll look at you any way
I want, you're my wife.

898
00:34:13,215 --> 00:34:14,430
- [Joel] Man, I do
wonder what it'd be like

899
00:34:14,430 --> 00:34:16,953
to make love to you, though.

900
00:34:16,953 --> 00:34:18,564
- [Crow] No, don't do that, lady!

901
00:34:18,564 --> 00:34:20,064
- [Tom] It's okay.

902
00:34:22,917 --> 00:34:24,290
Oh, Mommy!

903
00:34:24,290 --> 00:34:25,957
- [Joel] Honey West.

904
00:34:26,929 --> 00:34:29,194
- [Crow] Joel, I thought
underwear was supposed to match.

905
00:34:29,194 --> 00:34:30,685
- Who do you think you're talking to?

906
00:34:30,685 --> 00:34:33,026
"Don't touch me." You're my wife.

907
00:34:33,026 --> 00:34:34,717
I'll touch you any time I feel like it.

908
00:34:34,717 --> 00:34:37,574
- [Crow] You must have
a real good personality.

909
00:34:37,574 --> 00:34:40,324
- [Joel] Can I call you sometime?

910
00:34:42,356 --> 00:34:44,202
- Where you going?

911
00:34:44,202 --> 00:34:45,260
(piano music)

912
00:34:45,260 --> 00:34:48,227
- [Tom] A fellow named Humbert
Humbert wants to see me.

913
00:34:48,227 --> 00:34:49,727
- Where you going?

914
00:34:50,836 --> 00:34:51,873
- I'm going out.

915
00:34:51,873 --> 00:34:52,975
- [Joel] To church.

916
00:34:52,975 --> 00:34:56,507
- Maybe I'll be back, maybe I won't.

917
00:34:56,507 --> 00:34:57,489
- Liz, baby.

918
00:34:57,489 --> 00:34:58,708
- [Crow] Do I have to guess?

919
00:34:58,708 --> 00:35:00,645
Do I get a prize if I can guess?

920
00:35:00,645 --> 00:35:03,787
- [Joel] Can I eat your dinner then?

921
00:35:03,787 --> 00:35:04,657
- [Tom] Shoudn't have married her

922
00:35:04,657 --> 00:35:06,234
knowing that she loved me.

923
00:35:06,234 --> 00:35:08,984
(frogs croaking)

924
00:35:13,734 --> 00:35:17,133
- [Crow] How come we're not moving?

925
00:35:17,133 --> 00:35:19,648
(vomiting sounds)

926
00:35:19,648 --> 00:35:21,738
- [Crow] Okay, all done.

927
00:35:21,738 --> 00:35:22,571
(grunt)

928
00:35:22,571 --> 00:35:25,988
- [Joel] Hey, it's Jim from Wild Kingdom.

929
00:35:27,600 --> 00:35:28,818
- Well, there was a young one

930
00:35:28,818 --> 00:35:30,466
in the last trap, I had to kill it.

931
00:35:30,466 --> 00:35:31,547
- Oh, poor thing.

932
00:35:31,547 --> 00:35:32,600
- [Crow] Yeah, boo-hoo.

933
00:35:32,600 --> 00:35:34,082
- Told you, you shouldn't have come along.

934
00:35:34,082 --> 00:35:37,115
- [Tom] Well, let's have some kids.

935
00:35:37,115 --> 00:35:40,012
- [Joel] But I love sitting
in a smelly pickup truck.

936
00:35:40,012 --> 00:35:41,364
- I can understand hunting an animal,

937
00:35:41,364 --> 00:35:44,185
but making it suffer in those traps...

938
00:35:44,185 --> 00:35:46,626
- [Crow] Hey, I suffer
in the trap of love.

939
00:35:46,626 --> 00:35:47,459
- These poachers know the swamps

940
00:35:47,459 --> 00:35:48,629
like the back of their hands.

941
00:35:48,629 --> 00:35:50,428
But I'll catch one in the act some night

942
00:35:50,428 --> 00:35:51,948
and put him away for six months.

943
00:35:51,948 --> 00:35:53,072
- [Crow] My, he's rugged.

944
00:35:53,072 --> 00:35:54,262
- In the meantime, I'll
have to be satisfied

945
00:35:54,262 --> 00:35:55,875
confiscating their trap lines.

946
00:35:55,875 --> 00:35:56,864
- [Tom] That's reward enough.

947
00:35:56,864 --> 00:35:58,613
- Lose enough traps, it won't
pay them to keep trying.

948
00:35:58,613 --> 00:36:00,218
- Be careful, darling.

949
00:36:00,218 --> 00:36:01,873
These people aren't like other folks.

950
00:36:01,873 --> 00:36:03,340
They'll try and get back at you.

951
00:36:03,340 --> 00:36:05,525
- [Joel] They've got
seven toes on each foot.

952
00:36:05,525 --> 00:36:07,658
- Steve, you're a stranger here.

953
00:36:07,658 --> 00:36:09,625
They've lived this way for generations.

954
00:36:09,625 --> 00:36:11,870
One man isn't gonna change them overnight.

955
00:36:11,870 --> 00:36:13,539
- [Crow] Yeah, they gotta wanna change.

956
00:36:13,539 --> 00:36:17,436
- I'm serious, Steve,
they can be dangerous.

957
00:36:17,436 --> 00:36:19,794
- Okay, I'll be careful.

958
00:36:19,794 --> 00:36:22,090
You just stop worrying.

959
00:36:22,090 --> 00:36:23,257
- [Tom] Uh-huh.

960
00:36:23,257 --> 00:36:24,920
- [Crow] Oh, he's the killer leech.

961
00:36:24,920 --> 00:36:27,410
- [Tom] No, lech. It's the
attack of the killer lech.

962
00:36:27,410 --> 00:36:30,453
- (sigh) Oh, Steve, I love you.

963
00:36:30,453 --> 00:36:32,604
I love you so much.

964
00:36:32,604 --> 00:36:36,557
♫ I don't know how to (muffled) ♫

965
00:36:36,557 --> 00:36:37,826
(distant screaming)

966
00:36:37,826 --> 00:36:38,988
- What was that?

967
00:36:38,988 --> 00:36:41,521
- I don't know, you get in
the truck and lock it up.

968
00:36:41,521 --> 00:36:42,901
- [Tom] Sounded like Hee Haw.

969
00:36:42,901 --> 00:36:45,996
- [Crow] Could you leave the Mad Dog here?

970
00:36:45,996 --> 00:36:48,883
- [Joel] She dresses pretty
formally for a leech hunt.

971
00:36:48,883 --> 00:36:51,216
(screaming)

972
00:36:54,072 --> 00:36:58,239
- [Tom] Becky, we were
just playing a little game.

973
00:36:59,745 --> 00:37:01,157
(dramatic music)

974
00:37:01,157 --> 00:37:05,268
- [Crow] Oh, thanks for
comforting me with a gun.

975
00:37:05,268 --> 00:37:06,268
- [Tom] Ugh.

976
00:37:08,366 --> 00:37:10,731
- [Joel] Uh, he's a mess.

977
00:37:10,731 --> 00:37:12,126
A chocolate mess.

978
00:37:12,126 --> 00:37:12,959
(lip smacking)

979
00:37:12,959 --> 00:37:17,126
(Mystery Science Theater 3000 theme)

980
00:37:18,463 --> 00:37:21,627
- [Crow] Mmm, breakfast beans.

981
00:37:21,627 --> 00:37:24,183
- [Joel] Aw, this guy's
the sheriff of everything.

982
00:37:24,183 --> 00:37:26,697
- The coroner ruled that
Sawyer died in a misadventure.

983
00:37:26,697 --> 00:37:28,975
As far as this officer's
concerned, that's the end of it.

984
00:37:28,975 --> 00:37:30,692
- [Tom] That's a common cause of death.

985
00:37:30,692 --> 00:37:32,722
- If Lem was killed by anything human,

986
00:37:32,722 --> 00:37:34,709
I wouldn't need you to tell me my job.

987
00:37:34,709 --> 00:37:37,235
- [Crow] Ah, shut up
and finish your Kaboom.

988
00:37:37,235 --> 00:37:39,145
- ...looking for an overgrown gator.

989
00:37:39,145 --> 00:37:40,597
- [Tom] What do I look like, a cop?

990
00:37:40,597 --> 00:37:42,088
- Sawyer wasn't killed by an alligator.

991
00:37:42,088 --> 00:37:43,052
- Is that so?

992
00:37:43,052 --> 00:37:44,730
Did you hear that, Morton?

993
00:37:44,730 --> 00:37:47,813
Game warden Benton says it
wasn't a gator that killed Lem.

994
00:37:47,813 --> 00:37:49,266
Well then, what was it?

995
00:37:49,266 --> 00:37:51,486
You know so much, go on,
tell me, what killed him?

996
00:37:51,486 --> 00:37:53,185
- [Joel] It was low level stress, Troy.

997
00:37:53,185 --> 00:37:54,490
- The wounds were the kind a squid

998
00:37:54,490 --> 00:37:56,308
or a large octopus might cause.

999
00:37:56,308 --> 00:37:57,265
- [Crow] I love squid!

1000
00:37:57,265 --> 00:38:00,324
- Sure, did Greyson explain
how a saltwater creature

1001
00:38:00,324 --> 00:38:03,840
happened to be 30 miles
inland living in clear water?

1002
00:38:03,840 --> 00:38:04,899
- Stranger things have happened.

1003
00:38:04,899 --> 00:38:08,142
- Look here, Benton,
you work for the state.

1004
00:38:08,142 --> 00:38:09,027
- [Crow] The state of confusion.

1005
00:38:09,027 --> 00:38:11,289
- Why don't you keep your
nose out of county business?

1006
00:38:11,289 --> 00:38:14,054
Is that clear enough for you?

1007
00:38:14,054 --> 00:38:15,999
- Oh, go soak your fat head.

1008
00:38:15,999 --> 00:38:17,851
- [Joel] Okay then, well
I'll go get some soap and...

1009
00:38:17,851 --> 00:38:19,450
Hey, wait a minute!

1010
00:38:19,450 --> 00:38:20,805
- What was that you said?

1011
00:38:20,805 --> 00:38:22,783
- I said go soak your fat head.

1012
00:38:22,783 --> 00:38:24,600
- [Tom] Is that exactly
how you phrased it?

1013
00:38:24,600 --> 00:38:26,117
Wasn't there more of a lilting quality?

1014
00:38:26,117 --> 00:38:26,950
Gah!

1015
00:38:28,222 --> 00:38:32,660
- [Joel] Why if I wasn't so
stuffed with beans right now.

1016
00:38:32,660 --> 00:38:33,709
- He's sure going to get it.

1017
00:38:33,709 --> 00:38:35,098
(burp)

1018
00:38:35,098 --> 00:38:36,158
You can bet on that.

1019
00:38:36,158 --> 00:38:39,425
- [Crow] I thought it was
funny, Sheriff. (laughs)

1020
00:38:39,425 --> 00:38:43,207
- [Tom] Gary Busey, ladies
and gentlemen, Gary Busey.

1021
00:38:43,207 --> 00:38:44,926
- [Crow] Look, I sneezed on my dictionary.

1022
00:38:44,926 --> 00:38:47,063
- You see, the octopi
uses its suction discs

1023
00:38:47,063 --> 00:38:48,403
to hold its prey.

1024
00:38:48,403 --> 00:38:49,822
- [Crow] Like I hold a sandwich.

1025
00:38:49,822 --> 00:38:51,742
- The disc will not inflict a wound.

1026
00:38:51,742 --> 00:38:53,412
Same is true with a squid.

1027
00:38:53,412 --> 00:38:55,612
Now both creatures have a parrot-like beak

1028
00:38:55,612 --> 00:38:58,541
as their primary offensive
and defensive weapon.

1029
00:38:58,541 --> 00:39:00,259
- Maybe the sheriff was right, Dad.

1030
00:39:00,259 --> 00:39:02,076
- [Joel] I think I know
where the drapes went.

1031
00:39:02,076 --> 00:39:04,745
- Could be a freak or
a malformed alligator.

1032
00:39:04,745 --> 00:39:06,707
- [Crow] Thanks for the
coffee, Mrs. Cleavage.

1033
00:39:06,707 --> 00:39:09,355
- No, I'm afraid it wasn't.

1034
00:39:09,355 --> 00:39:11,697
- Well where does that leave us, Doc?

1035
00:39:11,697 --> 00:39:12,530
- [Tom] In the living room.

1036
00:39:12,530 --> 00:39:13,697
- Wish I knew.

1037
00:39:14,712 --> 00:39:15,565
You know, I'm beginning to think

1038
00:39:15,565 --> 00:39:18,581
Lem was telling the
truth in Walker's store.

1039
00:39:18,581 --> 00:39:21,012
He did see something that night.

1040
00:39:21,012 --> 00:39:22,624
Something that had intelligence enough

1041
00:39:22,624 --> 00:39:24,565
to seek him out and destroy him later.

1042
00:39:24,565 --> 00:39:25,398
- [Crow] That was smart.

1043
00:39:25,398 --> 00:39:27,295
- Sawyer claims to have
killed the thing he saw.

1044
00:39:27,295 --> 00:39:29,847
Put a half a dozen rifle bullets into it.

1045
00:39:29,847 --> 00:39:31,644
I've seen that old 44-40 he used.

1046
00:39:31,644 --> 00:39:33,809
Nothing could live after
being hit with those slugs.

1047
00:39:33,809 --> 00:39:34,702
- [Joel] Only half a cup?

1048
00:39:34,702 --> 00:39:36,309
Don't I like my coffee?

1049
00:39:36,309 --> 00:39:39,260
- Conceding the possibility
of one such creature,

1050
00:39:39,260 --> 00:39:42,411
we must also concede the
possibility of others.

1051
00:39:42,411 --> 00:39:43,356
- [Tom] Oh, sorry, you lost me.

1052
00:39:43,356 --> 00:39:44,529
You know, this coffee's wonderful.

1053
00:39:44,529 --> 00:39:46,990
- There's one sure way of finding out.

1054
00:39:46,990 --> 00:39:48,710
Go in and search every backwater channel

1055
00:39:48,710 --> 00:39:50,445
and wet spot in the swamp.

1056
00:39:50,445 --> 00:39:51,335
- [Crow] Any volunteers?

1057
00:39:51,335 --> 00:39:52,734
- If it's there, I'll find it.

1058
00:39:52,734 --> 00:39:54,125
- [Joel] It all points
to one thing, Drake.

1059
00:39:54,125 --> 00:39:55,792
This coffee's grand.

1060
00:39:58,145 --> 00:40:02,456
- [Tom] ♫ From the land
of sky blue water ♫

1061
00:40:02,456 --> 00:40:05,206
(dramatic music)

1062
00:40:06,222 --> 00:40:07,055
Aw, heck, honey.

1063
00:40:07,055 --> 00:40:07,888
I got a motor on this thing.

1064
00:40:07,888 --> 00:40:09,388
I just remembered.

1065
00:40:11,473 --> 00:40:14,125
- [Crow] Do you know any other camp songs?

1066
00:40:14,125 --> 00:40:15,891
- [Tom] Let's see, Barnacle Bill?

1067
00:40:15,891 --> 00:40:17,425
- [Joel] Yeah, watever you do,

1068
00:40:17,425 --> 00:40:20,775
don't help me paddle, Miss Dead Weight.

1069
00:40:20,775 --> 00:40:25,057
- [Tom] Well you know, a
visor might've been helpful.

1070
00:40:25,057 --> 00:40:28,727
- [Crow] It's a swamp, should
we bring our evil in here?

1071
00:40:28,727 --> 00:40:31,477
(dramatic music)

1072
00:40:35,721 --> 00:40:38,990
- [Tom] Charlie, there's
leeches all over your back.

1073
00:40:38,990 --> 00:40:41,952
- It sure is quiet around here.

1074
00:40:41,952 --> 00:40:46,192
- [Joel] What is she, on her
lunch break from Wendy's?

1075
00:40:46,192 --> 00:40:50,359
- [Crow] ♫ Wreck of
the Edmund Fitzgerald ♫

1076
00:40:52,272 --> 00:40:54,204
- [Joel] Here, leechy, leechy, leechy.

1077
00:40:54,204 --> 00:40:57,287
Come on, nice leechy, leechy, leechy.

1078
00:40:58,388 --> 00:40:59,392
- [Crow] I'll tell you
one thing right now.

1079
00:40:59,392 --> 00:41:02,230
I am not going to portage this thing.

1080
00:41:02,230 --> 00:41:03,790
- [Tom] You know, maybe
this isn't the right time

1081
00:41:03,790 --> 00:41:05,640
to talk about this, but I love muffins.

1082
00:41:05,640 --> 00:41:08,404
You know those real fancy
nut and fruit muffins

1083
00:41:08,404 --> 00:41:10,419
they got, full of whole grain?

1084
00:41:10,419 --> 00:41:11,969
- [Joel] Simon, no!

1085
00:41:11,969 --> 00:41:13,386
- [Tom] The heck?

1086
00:41:14,517 --> 00:41:15,587
- Did you hear something?

1087
00:41:15,587 --> 00:41:19,754
- [Crow] Um, I heard you say,
"Did you hear something?"

1088
00:41:21,241 --> 00:41:23,430
- Well, whatever it was,
it's not here anymore.

1089
00:41:23,430 --> 00:41:27,334
- [Tom] Probably over
the state line by now.

1090
00:41:27,334 --> 00:41:28,167
- [Joel] Be quiet.

1091
00:41:28,167 --> 00:41:31,183
Phil Harris and Curt Gowdy
are duck hunting back there.

1092
00:41:31,183 --> 00:41:32,217
- Do you want some coffee?

1093
00:41:32,217 --> 00:41:33,494
- [Crow] Again with the coffee!

1094
00:41:33,494 --> 00:41:35,911
Well, it is very good coffee.

1095
00:41:37,468 --> 00:41:40,042
- An Army could hide in
here and never be noticed.

1096
00:41:40,042 --> 00:41:41,519
- One did.

1097
00:41:41,519 --> 00:41:42,895
- [Nan] What?

1098
00:41:42,895 --> 00:41:44,702
The Seminoles under Osceola in the 1830s.

1099
00:41:44,702 --> 00:41:46,016
- [Tom] Aw gee, I was just
trying to break the ice.

1100
00:41:46,016 --> 00:41:48,588
I don't need a history lesson.

1101
00:41:48,588 --> 00:41:51,548
- I remember reading about
Osceola in high school.

1102
00:41:51,548 --> 00:41:52,402
- [Crow] Yeah, those Seminoles.

1103
00:41:52,402 --> 00:41:55,050
Boy, they drink a lot of coffee.

1104
00:41:55,050 --> 00:41:57,984
- I've only been at it two
days and I feel worn out.

1105
00:41:57,984 --> 00:41:59,571
- [Joel] Probably 'cause of my stories.

1106
00:41:59,571 --> 00:42:03,064
- Might as well keep on looking around.

1107
00:42:03,064 --> 00:42:05,761
- [Tom] Okay, well then,
like some more coffee?

1108
00:42:05,761 --> 00:42:06,840
I'll get you some more coffee.

1109
00:42:06,840 --> 00:42:08,133
Dinner's ready back here.

1110
00:42:08,133 --> 00:42:09,482
Do you want Rice-A-Roni?

1111
00:42:09,482 --> 00:42:10,971
There's some steak tartare

1112
00:42:10,971 --> 00:42:12,904
and some little O'Brien potatoes.

1113
00:42:12,904 --> 00:42:14,049
- [Crow] Hey, what's this hole in the...

1114
00:42:14,049 --> 00:42:15,266
Augh!

1115
00:42:15,266 --> 00:42:18,266
(suspenseful music)

1116
00:42:21,032 --> 00:42:23,078
- [Joel] I feel like Lewis and Clark.

1117
00:42:23,078 --> 00:42:23,911
- [Crow] Really?

1118
00:42:23,911 --> 00:42:27,531
- [Joel] You be Jerry
Lewis, I'll be Petula Clark.

1119
00:42:27,531 --> 00:42:30,531
(suspenseful music)

1120
00:42:32,129 --> 00:42:34,181
- Well, I guess we may as
well give up for the day.

1121
00:42:34,181 --> 00:42:35,934
- You won't get any arguments from me.

1122
00:42:35,934 --> 00:42:37,046
- [Crow] Okay, let's go.

1123
00:42:37,046 --> 00:42:38,181
- [Tom] But I don't wanna go now.

1124
00:42:38,181 --> 00:42:39,187
- [Crow] What?

1125
00:42:39,187 --> 00:42:40,567
- [Tom] Will the leech survive?

1126
00:42:40,567 --> 00:42:41,782
Will they drink more coffee?

1127
00:42:41,782 --> 00:42:44,291
And will the fat guy's
wife ever touch him?

1128
00:42:44,291 --> 00:42:45,782
And what about Naomi?

1129
00:42:45,782 --> 00:42:47,282
- [Crow] Hi there.

1130
00:42:48,783 --> 00:42:52,313
- [Joel] Look, either pick
up a paddle or sit still.

1131
00:42:52,313 --> 00:42:55,679
(suspenseful music)

1132
00:42:55,679 --> 00:42:57,242
Is it asking too much to pull over

1133
00:42:57,242 --> 00:43:00,033
to a station and ask for directions?

1134
00:43:00,033 --> 00:43:00,950
- [Tom] No.

1135
00:43:02,345 --> 00:43:03,933
Can we do something different
next time we go out,

1136
00:43:03,933 --> 00:43:06,715
like go to a restaurant or something?

1137
00:43:06,715 --> 00:43:07,548
- [Crow] Hello again.

1138
00:43:07,548 --> 00:43:08,381
I'm the leech.

1139
00:43:08,381 --> 00:43:09,548
See you later.

1140
00:43:11,115 --> 00:43:12,383
Well, let's see, that'll hold me

1141
00:43:12,383 --> 00:43:14,398
'til I get to the office anyway.

1142
00:43:14,398 --> 00:43:16,536
- [Joel] ♫ Doin' the Big Fig Newton

1143
00:43:16,536 --> 00:43:19,898
♫ Here's the tricky part,
the Big Fig Newton ♫

1144
00:43:19,898 --> 00:43:22,315
- I'm asking you for the
last time, Dave Walker.

1145
00:43:22,315 --> 00:43:23,882
- [Tom] Did you eat the last moon pie?

1146
00:43:23,882 --> 00:43:25,482
- Will you answer me, you dirty old man?

1147
00:43:25,482 --> 00:43:26,315
- [Crow] Dirty old man?

1148
00:43:26,315 --> 00:43:27,784
The ropers get more action than I...

1149
00:43:27,784 --> 00:43:30,026
- I ain't dirty, Liz, baby.

1150
00:43:30,026 --> 00:43:31,859
- Liz baby, Liz honey.

1151
00:43:33,631 --> 00:43:35,534
Can't you think of anything
else to say, stupid?

1152
00:43:35,534 --> 00:43:37,594
- Gotta get this order
over to Miss Sholby.

1153
00:43:37,594 --> 00:43:39,557
She's one of my better customers.

1154
00:43:39,557 --> 00:43:43,813
- [Crow] You know, I think
the bloom's off the rose.

1155
00:43:43,813 --> 00:43:45,026
- I love you, Liz, baby.

1156
00:43:45,026 --> 00:43:47,395
- [Tom] But I love groceries more.

1157
00:43:47,395 --> 00:43:48,978
- Get out.

1158
00:43:48,978 --> 00:43:50,274
Get out, you fat pig!

1159
00:43:50,274 --> 00:43:51,107
- [Joel] Now wait a minute.

1160
00:43:51,107 --> 00:43:55,274
I don't go calling you a
beautiful shapely woman, do I?

1161
00:43:58,364 --> 00:43:59,671
- [Tom] Oh, forgot my hat.

1162
00:43:59,671 --> 00:44:01,436
(cans clacking)

1163
00:44:01,436 --> 00:44:03,954
- [Crow] Cleanup on aisle three, please.

1164
00:44:03,954 --> 00:44:07,036
(door slams shut)

1165
00:44:07,036 --> 00:44:10,858
- [Joel] Listen, don't even ask, alright?

1166
00:44:10,858 --> 00:44:12,510
- You know women, Cal.

1167
00:44:12,510 --> 00:44:15,585
Gotta get their hair
down every now and then.

1168
00:44:15,585 --> 00:44:17,925
- You sure do know an
awful lot about females.

1169
00:44:17,925 --> 00:44:20,964
- [Tom] Well, I was a female once.

1170
00:44:20,964 --> 00:44:23,163
- I can't stand here and jaunt, Cal.

1171
00:44:23,163 --> 00:44:26,660
- [Crow] Gotta save my jaw for eating.

1172
00:44:26,660 --> 00:44:28,863
- [Tom] Here you go, Miss Sholby.

1173
00:44:28,863 --> 00:44:29,696
- Well...

1174
00:44:31,260 --> 00:44:33,618
ever since Lem got himself killed,

1175
00:44:33,618 --> 00:44:36,196
she wants me to sell
out and move into town.

1176
00:44:36,196 --> 00:44:37,328
- [Joel] And leave all this?

1177
00:44:37,328 --> 00:44:39,857
- Don't you let her bamboozle you, Dave.

1178
00:44:39,857 --> 00:44:42,849
You belong right here in the store.

1179
00:44:42,849 --> 00:44:45,260
After all, place wouldn't be
the same without you around.

1180
00:44:45,260 --> 00:44:46,914
- [Crow] Well, there'd be more
oxygen for the rest of us.

1181
00:44:46,914 --> 00:44:47,863
- Thanks, Cal.

1182
00:44:47,863 --> 00:44:49,242
I better get going.

1183
00:44:49,242 --> 00:44:50,832
See you when I get back.

1184
00:44:50,832 --> 00:44:52,750
- Yeah, I'll be around.

1185
00:44:52,750 --> 00:44:54,491
- [Tom] Ah, what a rich life, huh?

1186
00:44:54,491 --> 00:44:57,314
Flipping a walnut in a sieve all day.

1187
00:44:57,314 --> 00:45:01,481
- [Joel] Heh heh, now I got
my pick of the wicker baskets.

1188
00:45:02,576 --> 00:45:05,460
(peppy music)

1189
00:45:05,460 --> 00:45:06,792
- [Crow] Hey, where are
your outboard motor...

1190
00:45:06,792 --> 00:45:07,625
Oh, there they are.

1191
00:45:07,625 --> 00:45:09,398
- Liz, baby.

1192
00:45:09,398 --> 00:45:11,070
- [Tom] Cora?

1193
00:45:11,070 --> 00:45:12,426
(metal banging)

1194
00:45:12,426 --> 00:45:13,999
- [Joel] Yeah, I'm here about the affair.

1195
00:45:13,999 --> 00:45:16,517
I have an appointment
for 2 o'clock Wednesday.

1196
00:45:16,517 --> 00:45:20,267
- [Tom] Say, could you
show me some sundries?

1197
00:45:22,643 --> 00:45:24,442
- You want something, Cal?

1198
00:45:24,442 --> 00:45:27,859
- [Crow] Uh, do I still have a tab going?

1199
00:45:28,919 --> 00:45:30,312
- I sure do, honey.

1200
00:45:30,312 --> 00:45:33,903
- [Joel] Well, I'm real partial
to them Hormel dinner meals.

1201
00:45:33,903 --> 00:45:36,785
- [Tom] What, I'm not a mind reader.

1202
00:45:36,785 --> 00:45:37,868
- [Joel] Wow.

1203
00:45:38,747 --> 00:45:41,816
(peppy music)

1204
00:45:41,816 --> 00:45:44,384
(footsteps)

1205
00:45:44,384 --> 00:45:48,551
(Mystery Science Theater 3000 theme)

1206
00:45:50,270 --> 00:45:53,240
- Boy, some strong coffee,
I'm getting buzzed.

1207
00:45:53,240 --> 00:45:54,190
My neck plate's quivering

1208
00:45:54,190 --> 00:45:57,138
and my cute little acrylic
hands are shaking and sweaty.

1209
00:45:57,138 --> 00:45:58,357
Fill her up, Joel!

1210
00:45:58,357 --> 00:45:59,190
- Okay, I thought so.

1211
00:45:59,190 --> 00:46:00,989
Anything you say, little amigo.

1212
00:46:00,989 --> 00:46:02,022
- More sugar, please!

1213
00:46:02,022 --> 00:46:04,223
- Okay, I'm right on
it, Gypsy, no problem.

1214
00:46:04,223 --> 00:46:05,853
- Oy, Joel, today's experiment's got bite.

1215
00:46:05,853 --> 00:46:07,546
It's like...

1216
00:46:07,546 --> 00:46:09,129
- It's like a fever dream, Crow.

1217
00:46:09,129 --> 00:46:11,311
Or like one of those dreams
when you get home at midnight

1218
00:46:11,311 --> 00:46:13,573
and you wolf down the
Ukrainian sausage pastry

1219
00:46:13,573 --> 00:46:15,952
and a gross beer, then
you go straight to bed?

1220
00:46:15,952 --> 00:46:18,092
- Yeah, or when you just got
off a 12-hour shift at Arby's

1221
00:46:18,092 --> 00:46:19,485
and you're watching TV at 3am,

1222
00:46:19,485 --> 00:46:21,586
and then you fall asleep during Omega Man.

1223
00:46:21,586 --> 00:46:22,952
- Do you have any more half-and-half?

1224
00:46:22,952 --> 00:46:23,785
- No problem.

1225
00:46:23,785 --> 00:46:25,235
You know, I had a really
weird dream last night.

1226
00:46:25,235 --> 00:46:28,364
I dreamed that we all got back to Earth

1227
00:46:28,364 --> 00:46:30,973
and then you guys got a duplex together.

1228
00:46:30,973 --> 00:46:31,977
- You know, just last night,

1229
00:46:31,977 --> 00:46:33,847
I dreamed that I had a
really nice girlfriend

1230
00:46:33,847 --> 00:46:35,666
with red hair and a blue dress,

1231
00:46:35,666 --> 00:46:38,031
then she turned into a gargoyle
and I was really scared,

1232
00:46:38,031 --> 00:46:40,079
but then Jesus came and
everything was okay.

1233
00:46:40,079 --> 00:46:41,244
- You saw Jesus?

1234
00:46:41,244 --> 00:46:42,951
- Yeah. Real nice guy, too.

1235
00:46:42,951 --> 00:46:44,401
Thinner than you might think.

1236
00:46:44,401 --> 00:46:45,826
- I don't remember my
dreams too often, Joel.

1237
00:46:45,826 --> 00:46:47,313
- You know, I never could either,

1238
00:46:47,313 --> 00:46:48,966
until I started keeping a journal.

1239
00:46:48,966 --> 00:46:51,993
- Hey, I'm curious, what
do you dream about, Gypsy?

1240
00:46:51,993 --> 00:46:54,047
- I dream I fly in color.

1241
00:46:54,047 --> 00:46:56,146
- Oh, you dreamed you flew.

1242
00:46:56,146 --> 00:46:58,240
That means you're intuitive.

1243
00:46:58,240 --> 00:47:01,260
- I have one dream about a
little French café in Paris,

1244
00:47:01,260 --> 00:47:03,295
right off the route of Florentine.

1245
00:47:03,295 --> 00:47:05,426
What was that waiter's name?

1246
00:47:05,426 --> 00:47:06,717
- John Woo!

1247
00:47:06,717 --> 00:47:07,550
- You had that dream, too?

1248
00:47:07,550 --> 00:47:08,383
(alarm buzzing)

1249
00:47:08,383 --> 00:47:10,050
- We got movie sign!

1250
00:47:20,143 --> 00:47:22,642
- [Tom] Careful, I spilled
a little coffee right there.

1251
00:47:22,642 --> 00:47:24,880
- [Crow] I am wired.

1252
00:47:24,880 --> 00:47:26,393
(growling)

1253
00:47:26,393 --> 00:47:29,239
- I didn't hurt you now, did I?

1254
00:47:29,239 --> 00:47:31,176
- It's just, you're so strong.

1255
00:47:31,176 --> 00:47:33,468
- [Joel] Smelling, I mean.

1256
00:47:33,468 --> 00:47:35,086
- I like a man who's strong.

1257
00:47:35,086 --> 00:47:37,253
- [Crow] Smelling, I mean.

1258
00:47:38,484 --> 00:47:42,248
- You sure picked a doozy, that man, Dave.

1259
00:47:42,248 --> 00:47:44,623
I don't think he's got a muscle in him.

1260
00:47:44,623 --> 00:47:46,088
Just a big piece of flab.

1261
00:47:46,088 --> 00:47:48,505
- [Tom] You're talking
about the flab I love.

1262
00:47:48,505 --> 00:47:51,455
- Come on now, I didn't
mean anything against you.

1263
00:47:51,455 --> 00:47:53,344
- [Joel] I mean, you're
a little piece of flab.

1264
00:47:53,344 --> 00:47:56,639
- Just that I can't see
a real woman like you

1265
00:47:56,639 --> 00:47:58,689
tied up with a tub of lard like that.

1266
00:47:58,689 --> 00:48:02,439
- [Tom] Just can't imagine
you making love is all.

1267
00:48:02,439 --> 00:48:05,739
- You wouldn't understand, Cal.

1268
00:48:05,739 --> 00:48:08,656
You know, I wanted to tell you.

1269
00:48:08,656 --> 00:48:11,205
- [Crow] I'm the Ronald
McDonald for this region.

1270
00:48:11,205 --> 00:48:13,453
- You just wouldn't understand.

1271
00:48:13,453 --> 00:48:14,723
- Try me.

1272
00:48:14,723 --> 00:48:18,192
- [Tom] Free trial
sample, try me for a week.

1273
00:48:18,192 --> 00:48:20,775
- [Joel] Sister, you got roots.

1274
00:48:22,692 --> 00:48:23,525
- Huh?

1275
00:48:23,525 --> 00:48:24,358
- [Crow] Did you say something?

1276
00:48:24,358 --> 00:48:26,607
I wasn't listening.

1277
00:48:26,607 --> 00:48:27,569
- You crazy...

1278
00:48:27,569 --> 00:48:29,712
- [Joel] Kinda looks like a
forest up there in your nose.

1279
00:48:29,712 --> 00:48:31,080
- You could tell me you killed your pa

1280
00:48:31,080 --> 00:48:33,631
and your ma and your whole family.

1281
00:48:33,631 --> 00:48:35,275
I'll fight to protect you.

1282
00:48:35,275 --> 00:48:38,079
(birds chirping)

1283
00:48:38,079 --> 00:48:40,054
- [Crow] Ouch, my keys.

1284
00:48:40,054 --> 00:48:41,059
- [Tom] Hey, remember my pa

1285
00:48:41,059 --> 00:48:43,642
and my ma and the whole family?

1286
00:48:45,159 --> 00:48:47,192
- [Crow] We now return
to Fat Guy Goes Nutsoid,

1287
00:48:47,192 --> 00:48:48,618
a Tromo presentation.

1288
00:48:48,618 --> 00:48:50,680
- [Joel] He's using an adulterer decoy.

1289
00:48:50,680 --> 00:48:51,551
Come on, baby.

1290
00:48:51,551 --> 00:48:54,051
(kiss sounds)

1291
00:48:54,915 --> 00:48:56,499
- [Tom] Miss Sholby, I
got your groceries here.

1292
00:48:56,499 --> 00:48:59,815
Don't want no trouble this time.

1293
00:48:59,815 --> 00:49:02,764
- [Joel] Like that guy
could sneak up on somebody.

1294
00:49:02,764 --> 00:49:03,885
- [Tom] Honey?

1295
00:49:03,885 --> 00:49:07,281
Making out with one of
my friends back here?

1296
00:49:07,281 --> 00:49:10,725
- [Crow] Hmm, Swamp Blanket Bingo.

1297
00:49:10,725 --> 00:49:13,328
- [Joel] Let's see now,
there's some trees and a car

1298
00:49:13,328 --> 00:49:16,161
and my wife making out with... oh?

1299
00:49:17,024 --> 00:49:18,718
- First husband was a no-good bum.

1300
00:49:18,718 --> 00:49:20,309
- [Crow] Second husband, no-good bum.

1301
00:49:20,309 --> 00:49:22,559
Third husband, no-good bum.

1302
00:49:24,164 --> 00:49:25,332
- He used to get lushed up,

1303
00:49:25,332 --> 00:49:26,804
come home and beat up on me.

1304
00:49:26,804 --> 00:49:27,872
- [Crow] Oh, Merle Haggard.

1305
00:49:27,872 --> 00:49:28,705
- [Joel] Hey.

1306
00:49:28,705 --> 00:49:29,590
- [Crow] George Jones?

1307
00:49:29,590 --> 00:49:32,317
- Nothing but a prized pig.

1308
00:49:32,317 --> 00:49:34,714
- One night, he tried to
hold up a gas station.

1309
00:49:34,714 --> 00:49:36,720
- [Tom] But he wasn't very
strong and it fell on him.

1310
00:49:36,720 --> 00:49:39,475
- He was so drunk, he couldn't even run.

1311
00:49:39,475 --> 00:49:42,524
They caught him less than a mile away.

1312
00:49:42,524 --> 00:49:43,691
He got set up.

1313
00:49:45,130 --> 00:49:46,336
I got a divorce.

1314
00:49:46,336 --> 00:49:49,207
- [Tom] Then I dated David
Sole for a while and...

1315
00:49:49,207 --> 00:49:50,124
same thing.

1316
00:49:51,700 --> 00:49:52,896
- After three years with him...

1317
00:49:52,896 --> 00:49:55,063
(snoring)

1318
00:49:59,378 --> 00:50:02,813
- [Joel] Hey, she's
really opening up to him.

1319
00:50:02,813 --> 00:50:04,505
- I wish I could've been there.

1320
00:50:04,505 --> 00:50:08,034
I'd know how to take care
of a woman like you, Liz.

1321
00:50:08,034 --> 00:50:10,367
- Maybe it is too late, Cal.

1322
00:50:13,264 --> 00:50:17,074
- [Crow] Oh, good thing they
brought a make-out tarp.

1323
00:50:17,074 --> 00:50:18,608
- [Joel] I believe this is my dance.

1324
00:50:18,608 --> 00:50:20,331
- You're wrong, woman.

1325
00:50:20,331 --> 00:50:22,669
It's too late for the both of you.

1326
00:50:22,669 --> 00:50:24,230
- [Crow] Uh, she dropped
her contact lens in my mouth

1327
00:50:24,230 --> 00:50:25,689
and was trying to get
it out with her tongue.

1328
00:50:25,689 --> 00:50:26,522
Yeah, that's it.

1329
00:50:26,522 --> 00:50:27,855
- Go on, get up.

1330
00:50:29,044 --> 00:50:31,335
- Now look, Dave, I know
what you're thinking, but--

1331
00:50:31,335 --> 00:50:35,855
- I got double large
shot in this here thing.

1332
00:50:35,855 --> 00:50:39,256
You got two seconds
before I pull the trigger.

1333
00:50:39,256 --> 00:50:42,791
- [Tom] Okay, so I guess I'll
be seeing you there, huh?

1334
00:50:42,791 --> 00:50:44,547
- Don't get riled, Dave.

1335
00:50:44,547 --> 00:50:47,418
- [Joel] Just advancing
the plot, nothing personal.

1336
00:50:47,418 --> 00:50:49,763
- She's been pestering me
for months to take her out.

1337
00:50:49,763 --> 00:50:51,930
- Sure, Cal, I understand.

1338
00:50:53,422 --> 00:50:54,541
Get her up so I can get a better look

1339
00:50:54,541 --> 00:50:56,387
at the lying little tramp.

1340
00:50:56,387 --> 00:51:00,232
- [Joel] Which one of
us is the lying... oh.

1341
00:51:00,232 --> 00:51:01,498
- Come on, get up.

1342
00:51:01,498 --> 00:51:04,415
- No, he'll kill me, he'll kill me.

1343
00:51:05,691 --> 00:51:08,464
- Now your husband wants
to take a look at you.

1344
00:51:08,464 --> 00:51:10,464
- [Tom] Uh, is this her?

1345
00:51:12,062 --> 00:51:13,861
- You, uh, walk all the way, Dave?

1346
00:51:13,861 --> 00:51:15,746
- [Crow] No, there's a
motorcycle in my pants.

1347
00:51:15,746 --> 00:51:17,767
What do you think?

1348
00:51:17,767 --> 00:51:19,664
- Get over there!

1349
00:51:19,664 --> 00:51:22,262
- Now, Dave, it ain't as bad as you think.

1350
00:51:22,262 --> 00:51:24,583
- [Joel] It's fantastic.

1351
00:51:24,583 --> 00:51:25,416
- Tramp.

1352
00:51:26,691 --> 00:51:27,750
- What are you gonna do?

1353
00:51:27,750 --> 00:51:31,461
- [Joel] I'm gonna make
you listen to Supertramp.

1354
00:51:31,461 --> 00:51:32,747
- Dave, you put down that gun

1355
00:51:32,747 --> 00:51:34,257
before I get mad and make you eat it!

1356
00:51:34,257 --> 00:51:35,090
- [Tom] Eat it?

1357
00:51:35,090 --> 00:51:37,712
Hmm... hey, don't try to distract me.

1358
00:51:37,712 --> 00:51:39,350
- Go right ahead and try.

1359
00:51:39,350 --> 00:51:40,517
(gunshot)

1360
00:51:40,517 --> 00:51:41,419
- [Joel] I know what you're thinking.

1361
00:51:41,419 --> 00:51:44,086
Did I fire one shot or only one?

1362
00:51:45,886 --> 00:51:49,303
- [Tom] Every weekend, I go through this.

1363
00:51:51,500 --> 00:51:55,033
- [Crow] No, just wanted the tarp.

1364
00:51:55,033 --> 00:51:58,033
(suspenseful music)

1365
00:52:00,424 --> 00:52:01,300
(gunshot)

1366
00:52:01,300 --> 00:52:02,538
- [Joel] Now it's The Most Dangerous Game.

1367
00:52:02,538 --> 00:52:03,955
- [Tom] I'll say.

1368
00:52:04,941 --> 00:52:07,108
(gunshot)

1369
00:52:11,975 --> 00:52:13,714
- [Crow] This is more
like Night at the Iguana

1370
00:52:13,714 --> 00:52:16,447
than Attack of the Giant Leeches.

1371
00:52:16,447 --> 00:52:18,697
(grunting)

1372
00:52:20,855 --> 00:52:22,991
- [Tom] Quick, let's go
tell the camp councilor.

1373
00:52:22,991 --> 00:52:24,921
He'll help us.

1374
00:52:24,921 --> 00:52:25,754
- Come on!

1375
00:52:25,754 --> 00:52:26,587
(gunshot)

1376
00:52:26,587 --> 00:52:27,420
- I can't go any further!

1377
00:52:27,420 --> 00:52:30,253
- [Joel] I got nowhere else to go!

1378
00:52:32,372 --> 00:52:35,789
(heavy breathing)

1379
00:52:35,789 --> 00:52:36,622
- [Tom] (burp)

1380
00:52:36,622 --> 00:52:37,810
- [Crow] You two go on without me!

1381
00:52:37,810 --> 00:52:41,304
Oh, wait a minute, that's all wrong.

1382
00:52:41,304 --> 00:52:43,304
- [Tom] Thunder Husband.

1383
00:52:44,292 --> 00:52:48,192
- [Joel] I'll catch you,
you rascally adulterers.

1384
00:52:48,192 --> 00:52:50,239
(dramatic music)

1385
00:52:50,239 --> 00:52:53,589
Wait a minute, he's loading
his gun with Tootsie Rolls.

1386
00:52:53,589 --> 00:52:56,238
(lip smacking)

1387
00:52:56,238 --> 00:52:58,727
- Go on, run! Run 'til you drop!

1388
00:52:58,727 --> 00:53:00,477
- [Crow] Like I will!

1389
00:53:02,894 --> 00:53:05,132
- [Joel] Uh, let me
guess, she broke a heel?

1390
00:53:05,132 --> 00:53:09,900
- [Tom] Yeah, she's the
girl, she's supposed to.

1391
00:53:09,900 --> 00:53:10,946
- [Crow] Now what is with the mud?

1392
00:53:10,946 --> 00:53:13,113
Did we miss some metaphor?

1393
00:53:14,180 --> 00:53:17,347
- [Tom] Soil of the soul or something?

1394
00:53:18,929 --> 00:53:20,479
- [Joel] Heh heh, pull!

1395
00:53:20,479 --> 00:53:22,979
That one was just for me. Hah!

1396
00:53:24,220 --> 00:53:25,619
- I can't go on anymore, I can't.

1397
00:53:25,619 --> 00:53:26,886
You gotta let me stop
and rest for a minute.

1398
00:53:26,886 --> 00:53:28,202
- Shut up!

1399
00:53:28,202 --> 00:53:29,129
(gunshot)

1400
00:53:29,129 --> 00:53:31,794
- [Crow] Hey, that was
his heart blowing up.

1401
00:53:31,794 --> 00:53:33,461
- I can't go on, Cal.

1402
00:53:33,461 --> 00:53:36,211
(dramatic music)

1403
00:53:39,824 --> 00:53:41,032
- [Crow] There's gotta be a trailer park

1404
00:53:41,032 --> 00:53:43,138
around here somewhere.

1405
00:53:43,138 --> 00:53:44,058
- [Joel] Man, look at all the weed.

1406
00:53:44,058 --> 00:53:47,373
Where are they, Tommy Chong's backyard?

1407
00:53:47,373 --> 00:53:48,878
(gunshot)

1408
00:53:48,878 --> 00:53:51,374
- [Crow] Suddenly,
they're the defiant ones.

1409
00:53:51,374 --> 00:53:53,843
(silence)

1410
00:53:53,843 --> 00:53:56,426
- [Tom] (sigh)

1411
00:54:00,328 --> 00:54:02,411
- [Joel] Welp, guess you caught up to us.

1412
00:54:02,411 --> 00:54:04,123
- Go on, keep going.

1413
00:54:04,123 --> 00:54:07,239
- Dave, please, you gotta listen.

1414
00:54:07,239 --> 00:54:09,475
It wasn't my fault, honest.

1415
00:54:09,475 --> 00:54:12,108
She kept playing up to me every
time you turned your back.

1416
00:54:12,108 --> 00:54:13,272
It wasn't my fault.

1417
00:54:13,272 --> 00:54:14,560
- [Tom] I can't be held accountable.

1418
00:54:14,560 --> 00:54:16,588
She's got bosoms and everything.

1419
00:54:16,588 --> 00:54:18,307
- You and your muscles.

1420
00:54:18,307 --> 00:54:19,140
(spit)

1421
00:54:19,140 --> 00:54:20,710
- [Joel] Ew, she snagged on him.

1422
00:54:20,710 --> 00:54:22,889
- If it wasn't for you, I
wouldn't be in this fix.

1423
00:54:22,889 --> 00:54:25,932
And my old friend Dave wouldn't
be doing this thing to me.

1424
00:54:25,932 --> 00:54:27,442
It's all your fault!

1425
00:54:27,442 --> 00:54:28,707
- Oh! (spit)

1426
00:54:28,707 --> 00:54:29,709
- [Tom] She did it again.

1427
00:54:29,709 --> 00:54:30,784
- Get going.

1428
00:54:30,784 --> 00:54:32,072
- But Dave...

1429
00:54:32,072 --> 00:54:35,922
We've been friends a long time, Dave.

1430
00:54:35,922 --> 00:54:39,130
You wouldn't kill an old friend, Dave.

1431
00:54:39,130 --> 00:54:41,747
- If you don't move, I'll
kill you where you stand.

1432
00:54:41,747 --> 00:54:42,972
- But Dave!

1433
00:54:42,972 --> 00:54:45,065
(gunshot)

1434
00:54:45,065 --> 00:54:46,166
- He's crazy, man.

1435
00:54:46,166 --> 00:54:49,301
- [Joel] Did he just kill him or what?

1436
00:54:49,301 --> 00:54:51,065
(crying)

1437
00:54:51,065 --> 00:54:54,807
- [Crow] And as for you,
young lady, you're grounded.

1438
00:54:54,807 --> 00:54:56,592
- [Joel] They're melting.

1439
00:54:56,592 --> 00:54:58,675
(crying)

1440
00:55:02,154 --> 00:55:03,649
- Please, Dave!

1441
00:55:03,649 --> 00:55:04,482
- [Tom] I'm sorry, Dave.

1442
00:55:04,482 --> 00:55:06,386
Don't make us go to the deep end.

1443
00:55:06,386 --> 00:55:09,469
We're not ready for the deep end yet.

1444
00:55:10,701 --> 00:55:13,592
- [Crow] Hey, quit laughing,
I'm trying to humiliate you.

1445
00:55:13,592 --> 00:55:15,169
- I'm sorry, Dave!

1446
00:55:15,169 --> 00:55:17,502
I'm sorry, I didn't mean it!

1447
00:55:18,648 --> 00:55:22,143
- [Tom] Please, Dave, treat
me out to chicken corden bleu.

1448
00:55:22,143 --> 00:55:24,226
(crying)

1449
00:55:25,392 --> 00:55:26,225
- You think you learned enough

1450
00:55:26,225 --> 00:55:29,299
to stay away from my woman, Cal?

1451
00:55:29,299 --> 00:55:30,370
- [Tom] I learned plenty!

1452
00:55:30,370 --> 00:55:33,205
I got a PhD in staying
away from your wife!

1453
00:55:33,205 --> 00:55:36,036
- I'll do anything you
say, Dave, anything!

1454
00:55:36,036 --> 00:55:37,499
- [Joel] Would you rob a shop?

1455
00:55:37,499 --> 00:55:38,757
- [Tom] ♫ Anything ♫

1456
00:55:38,757 --> 00:55:40,417
- Come on out of there.

1457
00:55:40,417 --> 00:55:42,003
(dramatic music)

1458
00:55:42,003 --> 00:55:43,920
- Dave, give me a hand.

1459
00:55:45,577 --> 00:55:47,691
(deep growling)

1460
00:55:47,691 --> 00:55:49,358
- [Crow] Ay caramba!

1461
00:55:51,138 --> 00:55:52,876
- Look out, behind you!

1462
00:55:52,876 --> 00:55:55,021
(screaming)

1463
00:55:55,021 --> 00:55:55,938
- Oh, Dave!

1464
00:55:57,449 --> 00:56:00,085
- [Tom] Uh, don't help or anything, Dave.

1465
00:56:00,085 --> 00:56:01,087
(screaming)

1466
00:56:01,087 --> 00:56:05,201
- [Crow] Gee, it was okay to
shoot them, but this... yeck!

1467
00:56:05,201 --> 00:56:07,602
- [Tom] Think they know we're here?

1468
00:56:07,602 --> 00:56:10,031
(splashing)

1469
00:56:10,031 --> 00:56:11,730
- Ain't nothing down here, sir.

1470
00:56:11,730 --> 00:56:14,004
- [Joel] Except for these dead folks.

1471
00:56:14,004 --> 00:56:17,116
- Well, I'm getting tired of this.

1472
00:56:17,116 --> 00:56:18,834
Come on in, boys!

1473
00:56:18,834 --> 00:56:21,584
(birds chirping)

1474
00:56:22,585 --> 00:56:24,431
Now, if there was any bodies out there,

1475
00:56:24,431 --> 00:56:26,643
we'd have found them.

1476
00:56:26,643 --> 00:56:29,196
Now I want some straight answers, Walker.

1477
00:56:29,196 --> 00:56:30,356
And don't give me anymore

1478
00:56:30,356 --> 00:56:33,259
of that crud about monsters, neither.

1479
00:56:33,259 --> 00:56:35,175
Where'd you hide them
after you killed them?

1480
00:56:35,175 --> 00:56:37,555
- [Tom] (burp) Sorry.

1481
00:56:37,555 --> 00:56:39,775
- I told you the truth, Sheriff.

1482
00:56:39,775 --> 00:56:41,167
I didn't kill them.

1483
00:56:41,167 --> 00:56:42,952
- [Crow] I ate them.

1484
00:56:42,952 --> 00:56:45,620
- I just wanted to scare them good.

1485
00:56:45,620 --> 00:56:49,714
- [Tom] Don't cry, I hate it when you cry.

1486
00:56:49,714 --> 00:56:52,593
- I wouldn't do anything to hurt her.

1487
00:56:52,593 --> 00:56:53,779
I loved her.

1488
00:56:53,779 --> 00:56:56,030
- [Joel] I loved her more
than twice baked potatoes.

1489
00:56:56,030 --> 00:56:57,132
- You loved her.

1490
00:56:57,132 --> 00:57:00,120
That's why you chased her
through the swamp with a shotgun.

1491
00:57:00,120 --> 00:57:02,470
- [Crow] Well it's tough love, admittedly.

1492
00:57:02,470 --> 00:57:05,297
- [Sheriff] Where'd you hide the bodies?

1493
00:57:05,297 --> 00:57:06,844
- Won't you believe me?

1494
00:57:06,844 --> 00:57:08,813
It was them monsters, they--

1495
00:57:08,813 --> 00:57:12,431
- Take him out of here
before I lose my temper.

1496
00:57:12,431 --> 00:57:14,115
- [Tom] Gary Cooper in a cameo role.

1497
00:57:14,115 --> 00:57:17,350
Gary Cooper, ladies and
gentlemen, Gary Cooper.

1498
00:57:17,350 --> 00:57:18,664
(bird chirping)

1499
00:57:18,664 --> 00:57:19,962
- [Crow] Hey, it's Bartles and Jaymes.

1500
00:57:19,962 --> 00:57:23,343
- County pay a reward if someone
finds the bodies, Sheriff?

1501
00:57:23,343 --> 00:57:24,630
- [Tom] Reward.

1502
00:57:24,630 --> 00:57:26,170
- $50.

1503
00:57:26,170 --> 00:57:27,646
- [Joel] And you get to keep the body.

1504
00:57:27,646 --> 00:57:28,479
- Each?

1505
00:57:28,479 --> 00:57:30,089
- [Tom] For those extra genes you need.

1506
00:57:30,089 --> 00:57:31,534
- Yeah.

1507
00:57:31,534 --> 00:57:33,422
(monkey howling)

1508
00:57:33,422 --> 00:57:34,938
- [Tom] Sounds like Africa.

1509
00:57:34,938 --> 00:57:38,251
- You know, I never
thought that of ol' Dave.

1510
00:57:38,251 --> 00:57:40,091
Course, everybody know that Cal and Liz

1511
00:57:40,091 --> 00:57:41,720
had a hankering for each other.

1512
00:57:41,720 --> 00:57:43,412
- [Crow] I think it went
way beyond hankering.

1513
00:57:43,412 --> 00:57:45,175
- [Tom] Mhm.

1514
00:57:45,175 --> 00:57:46,788
- Darn fools.

1515
00:57:46,788 --> 00:57:49,705
Grappling out there in
the middle of the water.

1516
00:57:49,705 --> 00:57:51,668
If the bodies is still in there,

1517
00:57:51,668 --> 00:57:54,196
you can bet some old bull gator's got him

1518
00:57:54,196 --> 00:57:56,555
stuffed in his cave under the bank,

1519
00:57:56,555 --> 00:57:59,071
letting him ripen up for a few days.

1520
00:57:59,071 --> 00:58:02,753
- [Joel] Uh, Jeb, you
got a real dark side.

1521
00:58:02,753 --> 00:58:04,980
- What we need now is
some long cypress poles

1522
00:58:04,980 --> 00:58:06,134
to go in there and pull...

1523
00:58:06,134 --> 00:58:07,958
- [Crow] We'll drink
the pond dry, and then--

1524
00:58:07,958 --> 00:58:09,572
- [Tom] No, no.

1525
00:58:09,572 --> 00:58:12,241
- [Joel] Otis, your Quisp is ready.

1526
00:58:12,241 --> 00:58:14,794
(dramatic music)

1527
00:58:14,794 --> 00:58:15,916
- [Tom] He's stuck to the ceiling.

1528
00:58:15,916 --> 00:58:16,926
- [Joel] Great, a swinger.

1529
00:58:16,926 --> 00:58:18,062
Always on my shift.

1530
00:58:18,062 --> 00:58:19,858
- [Tom] For crying out loud.

1531
00:58:19,858 --> 00:58:22,755
(silence)

1532
00:58:22,755 --> 00:58:23,838
- [Tom] I just love these Hummels.

1533
00:58:23,838 --> 00:58:26,492
- I'm sorry, Doc, I can't do it.

1534
00:58:26,492 --> 00:58:28,058
- I'll have to do it myself.

1535
00:58:28,058 --> 00:58:29,454
- Wouldn't wanna have to
arrest you, Doc, but I will

1536
00:58:29,454 --> 00:58:32,157
if I find you near the
preserve with any explosives.

1537
00:58:32,157 --> 00:58:33,193
- Steve!

1538
00:58:33,193 --> 00:58:35,026
- Sorry, Nan, that's
the way it's gotta be.

1539
00:58:35,026 --> 00:58:36,192
- [Crow] I'm a jerk.

1540
00:58:36,192 --> 00:58:37,339
- Steve's right, Nan.

1541
00:58:37,339 --> 00:58:39,229
It's his job to protect wildlife.

1542
00:58:39,229 --> 00:58:41,959
- What about human life?

1543
00:58:41,959 --> 00:58:44,579
Three people have been
killed in that game preserve.

1544
00:58:44,579 --> 00:58:46,290
Doesn't that mean anything to you?

1545
00:58:46,290 --> 00:58:47,674
- Nan, try to understand.

1546
00:58:47,674 --> 00:58:48,752
- [Joel] I'm a jerk.

1547
00:58:48,752 --> 00:58:50,393
- If something in the
swamp was responsible

1548
00:58:50,393 --> 00:58:53,349
for those deaths, I
wouldn't hesitate a minute.

1549
00:58:53,349 --> 00:58:55,701
We've been together the last three days.

1550
00:58:55,701 --> 00:58:57,842
We didn't see a trace of anything unusual.

1551
00:58:57,842 --> 00:58:59,150
- [Crow] Except for that 10-foot leech.

1552
00:58:59,150 --> 00:59:00,533
- Not so much as a suggestion

1553
00:59:00,533 --> 00:59:02,665
of any form of life unknown to us.

1554
00:59:02,665 --> 00:59:03,498
- Steve!

1555
00:59:03,498 --> 00:59:05,344
- [Tom] Woah, Easy Lift needs adjusting.

1556
00:59:05,344 --> 00:59:06,776
- Alright, I'll admit Sawyer's death

1557
00:59:06,776 --> 00:59:09,246
left a lot of questions unanswered.

1558
00:59:09,246 --> 00:59:10,279
But you can bet your bottom dollar

1559
00:59:10,279 --> 00:59:12,906
the other two died from
Dave Walker's shotgun.

1560
00:59:12,906 --> 00:59:14,732
- Do you really believe that?

1561
00:59:14,732 --> 00:59:16,010
- Figure it out, Nan.

1562
00:59:16,010 --> 00:59:17,763
Walk admitted chasing his wife and Cal

1563
00:59:17,763 --> 00:59:19,670
into the swamps after
he found them together.

1564
00:59:19,670 --> 00:59:20,503
- [Joel] Cow?

1565
00:59:20,503 --> 00:59:21,413
He shot them and he tried

1566
00:59:21,413 --> 00:59:23,943
to place the blame on Sawyer's DTs.

1567
00:59:23,943 --> 00:59:26,692
If Sawyer told us seeing purple giraffes

1568
00:59:26,692 --> 00:59:28,346
with polka-dot tails walking around...

1569
00:59:28,346 --> 00:59:30,240
- [Tom] Woah, woah, back up a bit there.

1570
00:59:30,240 --> 00:59:32,370
- There's one argument
against that, Steve.

1571
00:59:32,370 --> 00:59:34,564
Dave Walker wasn't the kind
of a man to hang himself.

1572
00:59:34,564 --> 00:59:36,844
- [Crow] Nah, he's a blade man.

1573
00:59:36,844 --> 00:59:38,330
I talked with Dave less than an hour

1574
00:59:38,330 --> 00:59:40,427
before he killed himself.

1575
00:59:40,427 --> 00:59:42,378
- [Tom] Maybe that's why.

1576
00:59:42,378 --> 00:59:45,173
- I've seen frightened
men before, terrified men,

1577
00:59:45,173 --> 00:59:47,269
but I've never seen to
compare with the horror

1578
00:59:47,269 --> 00:59:49,799
and the fear in Walker's eyes.

1579
00:59:49,799 --> 00:59:51,041
- He realized what he'd done.

1580
00:59:51,041 --> 00:59:53,125
He killed two people, one his wife.

1581
00:59:53,125 --> 00:59:54,235
- [Joel] One, his Cal.

1582
00:59:54,235 --> 00:59:56,469
- Look, Doc, Nan and I
were all over that swamp.

1583
00:59:56,469 --> 00:59:57,302
- [Crow] And each other.

1584
00:59:57,302 --> 00:59:59,346
- [Steve] Why didn't
these monsters attack us?

1585
00:59:59,346 --> 01:00:01,026
- I've been thinking about that.

1586
01:00:01,026 --> 01:00:02,540
They're probably night creatures.

1587
01:00:02,540 --> 01:00:03,580
They can't stand the light.

1588
01:00:03,580 --> 01:00:05,327
They stay down near the
bottom during the day.

1589
01:00:05,327 --> 01:00:06,847
- [Tom] Like comedians.

1590
01:00:06,847 --> 01:00:08,338
- A small charge would stun them

1591
01:00:08,338 --> 01:00:10,309
and bring them to the surface.

1592
01:00:10,309 --> 01:00:11,809
- Can't bite, Doc.

1593
01:00:12,926 --> 01:00:14,396
- Pig-headed Steve Benton.

1594
01:00:14,396 --> 01:00:15,620
- [Joel] No, it's Steve-headed Pig Benton.

1595
01:00:15,620 --> 01:00:17,572
- Stubborn and pig-headed.

1596
01:00:17,572 --> 01:00:19,172
- You just don't understand, do you?

1597
01:00:19,172 --> 01:00:20,320
- [Tom] I'm a jerk!

1598
01:00:20,320 --> 01:00:21,671
- [Crow] Oh, I'll go make some coffee.

1599
01:00:21,671 --> 01:00:25,595
- [Joel] She doesn't
understand I'm a jerk, sir.

1600
01:00:25,595 --> 01:00:26,728
(door shuts)

1601
01:00:26,728 --> 01:00:27,709
- [Tom] Whatever.

1602
01:00:27,709 --> 01:00:28,765
- Goodnight, Doc.

1603
01:00:28,765 --> 01:00:30,777
- [Tom] Goodnight, jerk.

1604
01:00:30,777 --> 01:00:32,680
(suspenseful music)

1605
01:00:32,680 --> 01:00:33,513
- [Tom] I'm a beaver,

1606
01:00:33,513 --> 01:00:34,996
and this is the view from my living room.

1607
01:00:34,996 --> 01:00:35,990
Enjoy!

1608
01:00:35,990 --> 01:00:38,740
(dramatic music)

1609
01:00:40,157 --> 01:00:41,239
(low growling)

1610
01:00:41,239 --> 01:00:43,899
- [Crow] Hi, I'm back.

1611
01:00:43,899 --> 01:00:48,066
- [Tom] (singing in foreign language)

1612
01:00:50,302 --> 01:00:53,052
(water sloshing)

1613
01:00:58,569 --> 01:01:01,645
- [Tom] Got any Deep Woods Off there?

1614
01:01:01,645 --> 01:01:02,668
- [Crow] Well at least we's paddling

1615
01:01:02,668 --> 01:01:04,835
in the same direction now.

1616
01:01:05,828 --> 01:01:07,512
- Right over there in them reeds...

1617
01:01:07,512 --> 01:01:09,909
- [Joel] Is where my Daddy met my Pappy.

1618
01:01:09,909 --> 01:01:10,986
(low growling)

1619
01:01:10,986 --> 01:01:11,833
- [Tom] Wish we could see him.

1620
01:01:11,833 --> 01:01:14,399
You can't personalize a monster like this.

1621
01:01:14,399 --> 01:01:17,399
(suspenseful music)

1622
01:01:18,563 --> 01:01:21,422
- [Crow] Hey, look, a lot
of golf balls down in there.

1623
01:01:21,422 --> 01:01:23,845
(grunts)

1624
01:01:23,845 --> 01:01:25,819
- Just can't figure ol' Dave out.

1625
01:01:25,819 --> 01:01:27,649
- [Joel] He's a complex fellow.

1626
01:01:27,649 --> 01:01:30,089
- What would you do?

1627
01:01:30,089 --> 01:01:31,383
- I hadn't thought of that.

1628
01:01:31,383 --> 01:01:33,549
- [Joel] I hadn't thought
of anything, never.

1629
01:01:33,549 --> 01:01:35,454
- Right over there in them reeds...

1630
01:01:35,454 --> 01:01:39,693
- [Crow] Jerry Reed, Rex
Reed, Willis Reed, Donna Reed,

1631
01:01:39,693 --> 01:01:42,776
Lou Reed, Reed Richards, Robert Reed.

1632
01:01:44,399 --> 01:01:47,081
- [Tom] These guys are
inqesively incompetent.

1633
01:01:47,081 --> 01:01:50,134
(low growling)

1634
01:01:50,134 --> 01:01:53,568
- [Crow] Don't look now, I
think they found Jimmy Hoffa.

1635
01:01:53,568 --> 01:01:56,481
(silence)

1636
01:01:56,481 --> 01:01:59,418
- [Joel] Oh, take me to
the sweet mushroom palace,

1637
01:01:59,418 --> 01:02:00,970
my friend booze.

1638
01:02:00,970 --> 01:02:02,455
- There is sure a lot left.

1639
01:02:02,455 --> 01:02:06,583
- [Crow] That's the suntan lotion! (gags)

1640
01:02:06,583 --> 01:02:11,425
- [Joel] You ever feel like
crying and you don't know why?

1641
01:02:11,425 --> 01:02:12,891
- Sure is funny.

1642
01:02:12,891 --> 01:02:13,758
- Huh?

1643
01:02:13,758 --> 01:02:15,340
- [Tom] Gallagher. Said he sure is funny.

1644
01:02:15,340 --> 01:02:16,343
- Noticed anything?

1645
01:02:16,343 --> 01:02:17,176
- What?

1646
01:02:17,176 --> 01:02:19,672
- We've been prodding
around here all morning.

1647
01:02:19,672 --> 01:02:21,406
Ain't run across a single gator.

1648
01:02:21,406 --> 01:02:23,312
- [Joel] And I don't have no pants on.

1649
01:02:23,312 --> 01:02:26,413
- If I'm right, this pool
should be crawling with them.

1650
01:02:26,413 --> 01:02:29,615
- [Crow] I'm crawling with something.

1651
01:02:29,615 --> 01:02:31,285
- Yeah, that is funny.

1652
01:02:31,285 --> 01:02:32,731
- I don't like the looks of things.

1653
01:02:32,731 --> 01:02:34,933
- [Crow] And I don't
like the looks of you.

1654
01:02:34,933 --> 01:02:35,974
- Well, now wait a minute.

1655
01:02:35,974 --> 01:02:38,524
We ain't pulled the gator
holes around the bend yet.

1656
01:02:38,524 --> 01:02:40,461
- You do you what you wanna do, boy.

1657
01:02:40,461 --> 01:02:43,067
Just get me on dry land, right quick!

1658
01:02:43,067 --> 01:02:44,292
- [Joel] Don't ever get off the boat.

1659
01:02:44,292 --> 01:02:47,386
- No use getting all heated up. Let's go.

1660
01:02:47,386 --> 01:02:49,445
(water sloshing)

1661
01:02:49,445 --> 01:02:50,278
- [Joel] Alright, now make that

1662
01:02:50,278 --> 01:02:52,451
motor boat noise again, Caleb.

1663
01:02:52,451 --> 01:02:56,390
- [Tom] (motor boat noise)

1664
01:02:56,390 --> 01:02:58,079
- [Joel] By the way, can
I borrow your fondue set?

1665
01:02:58,079 --> 01:03:00,814
Sarah and her mother
are coming over later.

1666
01:03:00,814 --> 01:03:03,922
- Out here on a wild goose chase.

1667
01:03:03,922 --> 01:03:06,672
(dramatic music)

1668
01:03:09,353 --> 01:03:11,500
(leech roaring)

1669
01:03:11,500 --> 01:03:13,049
(screaming)

1670
01:03:13,049 --> 01:03:14,283
(splash)

1671
01:03:14,283 --> 01:03:15,383
- [Crow] This is the most fantastic

1672
01:03:15,383 --> 01:03:17,800
undersea odyssey ever filmed!

1673
01:03:18,650 --> 01:03:21,477
- [Tom] Oh, I just love
underwater love scenes.

1674
01:03:21,477 --> 01:03:23,676
(dramatic music)

1675
01:03:23,676 --> 01:03:25,170
(low growling)

1676
01:03:25,170 --> 01:03:27,180
Oh great, the leeches have a little fort.

1677
01:03:27,180 --> 01:03:28,256
- [Crow] Hello, Bond.

1678
01:03:28,256 --> 01:03:30,053
Sorry about the formality.

1679
01:03:30,053 --> 01:03:32,470
(low growls)

1680
01:03:35,752 --> 01:03:37,116
- [Tom] This one's a keeper.

1681
01:03:37,116 --> 01:03:38,928
We eat tonight.

1682
01:03:38,928 --> 01:03:43,095
- [Joel] You ever feel like
you're in a Gary Larson cartoon?

1683
01:03:44,649 --> 01:03:47,249
- [Tom] Leeches are just
guys in fancy ponchos.

1684
01:03:47,249 --> 01:03:49,582
- [Crow] They're not leeches,
they're scientologists.

1685
01:03:49,582 --> 01:03:51,522
- [Joel] They're Sears ponchos.

1686
01:03:51,522 --> 01:03:54,900
- [Tom] Oh look, Jim
Henson's Cracker Babies.

1687
01:03:54,900 --> 01:03:57,638
(screaming)

1688
01:03:57,638 --> 01:03:58,500
- [Crow] Hey, keep it down.

1689
01:03:58,500 --> 01:04:01,064
We're trying to sleep over here.

1690
01:04:01,064 --> 01:04:02,269
- [Joel] You still haven't got it right.

1691
01:04:02,269 --> 01:04:03,768
We don't want hillbillies with good taste.

1692
01:04:03,768 --> 01:04:05,434
We want hillbillies that taste good.

1693
01:04:05,434 --> 01:04:06,836
- [Crow] Hillbilly Red.

1694
01:04:06,836 --> 01:04:09,586
(dramatic music)

1695
01:04:13,324 --> 01:04:16,211
- [Tom] Honey, wake up, they're back.

1696
01:04:16,211 --> 01:04:19,283
- [Joel] Wow, he is gonna
have one immense hickey.

1697
01:04:19,283 --> 01:04:20,684
- [Crow] He's gonna have
to wear a turtleneck.

1698
01:04:20,684 --> 01:04:23,831
(screaming)

1699
01:04:23,831 --> 01:04:26,866
- [Joel] How am I gonna
explain this to my husband?

1700
01:04:26,866 --> 01:04:28,033
Ah, this side.

1701
01:04:28,931 --> 01:04:31,895
(low growling)

1702
01:04:31,895 --> 01:04:36,062
(Mystery Science Theater 3000 theme)

1703
01:04:37,664 --> 01:04:39,189
(dogs barking)

1704
01:04:39,189 --> 01:04:41,237
- [Tom] Aw, puppies.

1705
01:04:41,237 --> 01:04:44,999
- [Joel] ♫ You blown it all sky high ♫

1706
01:04:44,999 --> 01:04:45,832
You know, if I didn't know better,

1707
01:04:45,832 --> 01:04:48,300
I'd say we're in the dumbest
part of the movie right now.

1708
01:04:48,300 --> 01:04:49,280
- [Crow] Probably.

1709
01:04:49,280 --> 01:04:51,565
- [Joel] ♫ Without a reason why ♫

1710
01:04:51,565 --> 01:04:53,966
- [Crow] There's a madman
'cross the water there.

1711
01:04:53,966 --> 01:04:55,096
- [Joel] Buzz bomb reference.

1712
01:04:55,096 --> 01:04:56,062
- [Tom] Hey, Bob, you know I'm thinking

1713
01:04:56,062 --> 01:04:57,974
about getting me one of
them speedo swimsuits.

1714
01:04:57,974 --> 01:04:58,888
What do you think?

1715
01:04:58,888 --> 01:05:01,712
- [Joel] You'd look sweet, Pete.

1716
01:05:01,712 --> 01:05:03,166
Can we break for a snack?

1717
01:05:03,166 --> 01:05:05,765
Dave would've wanted us to.

1718
01:05:05,765 --> 01:05:09,432
- [Joel] We're under
here, you can't see us.

1719
01:05:11,096 --> 01:05:13,512
- [Tom] Oh, think my pants are shrinking.

1720
01:05:13,512 --> 01:05:15,380
(coughs)

1721
01:05:15,380 --> 01:05:16,337
(bubbles)

1722
01:05:16,337 --> 01:05:17,337
- All clear!

1723
01:05:19,252 --> 01:05:21,666
It's clear over here!

1724
01:05:21,666 --> 01:05:23,564
- [Crow] I'll be the judge of that.

1725
01:05:23,564 --> 01:05:24,965
(low growling)

1726
01:05:24,965 --> 01:05:26,863
(dog barking)

1727
01:05:26,863 --> 01:05:27,696
Hey, that's not a scent.

1728
01:05:27,696 --> 01:05:29,049
That's the other dog you're sniffing.

1729
01:05:29,049 --> 01:05:30,901
These dogs have been tracking each other!

1730
01:05:30,901 --> 01:05:33,465
- [Tom] Flibbity checkers, no.

1731
01:05:33,465 --> 01:05:35,578
- [Joel] Is it hot here or is it just me?

1732
01:05:35,578 --> 01:05:37,572
(grunts)

1733
01:05:37,572 --> 01:05:40,253
(suspenseful music)

1734
01:05:40,253 --> 01:05:41,511
- [Tom] Woah, outta here!

1735
01:05:41,511 --> 01:05:42,901
Feets, don't fail me now.

1736
01:05:42,901 --> 01:05:44,837
- [Joel] Stay together, cheeks.

1737
01:05:44,837 --> 01:05:47,997
(dogs growling)

1738
01:05:47,997 --> 01:05:51,497
- [Crow] Well, this scene isn't
panning out like I'd hoped.

1739
01:05:51,497 --> 01:05:54,997
- [Joel] Hey, spread out, this is my spot.

1740
01:05:58,140 --> 01:05:59,384
(dog howling)

1741
01:05:59,384 --> 01:06:01,303
- [Tom] Yeah yeah, we know,
you saw the best minds

1742
01:06:01,303 --> 01:06:03,701
of your generation destroyed
by madness, we know.

1743
01:06:03,701 --> 01:06:05,167
- Alright, let's keep moving.

1744
01:06:05,167 --> 01:06:06,874
- [Crow] To another film?

1745
01:06:06,874 --> 01:06:08,804
- [Joel] Champ, Rex, Spot, Otto.

1746
01:06:08,804 --> 01:06:10,540
(panting)

1747
01:06:10,540 --> 01:06:13,290
(dramatic music)

1748
01:06:15,869 --> 01:06:20,077
- [Tom] I claim this land
for the queen of Spain.

1749
01:06:20,077 --> 01:06:21,746
- [Joel] ♫ Wouldn't you like to glide

1750
01:06:21,746 --> 01:06:24,695
♫ In my beautiful balloon ♫

1751
01:06:24,695 --> 01:06:26,770
Man, they're really having
trouble with this scene.

1752
01:06:26,770 --> 01:06:28,438
Yeah, it's going nowhere.

1753
01:06:28,438 --> 01:06:29,553
- Well, we can't do anymore tonight.

1754
01:06:29,553 --> 01:06:30,803
Might as well start back.

1755
01:06:30,803 --> 01:06:33,939
- Reckon there ain't no use
hurrying now, Mr. Benton.

1756
01:06:33,939 --> 01:06:36,014
Even if Old Sam was alive,

1757
01:06:36,014 --> 01:06:39,069
they'd let us know with
a holler or something.

1758
01:06:39,069 --> 01:06:41,199
- Yeah, kinda looks that way.

1759
01:06:41,199 --> 01:06:43,185
- You fellas notice
anything about this lake?

1760
01:06:43,185 --> 01:06:44,826
- [Crow] Uh, it's wet?

1761
01:06:44,826 --> 01:06:45,817
- What about them?

1762
01:06:45,817 --> 01:06:46,885
- They ain't.

1763
01:06:46,885 --> 01:06:50,921
The water this side usually has
maybe 50, 60 big ones around

1764
01:06:50,921 --> 01:06:53,103
and a whole lot of little ones.

1765
01:06:53,103 --> 01:06:55,421
- [Tom] Well I could go
for a piece of one myself.

1766
01:06:55,421 --> 01:06:57,503
- Maybe our torches scared them away.

1767
01:06:57,503 --> 01:07:00,718
- Nothing scares gators, nothing.

1768
01:07:00,718 --> 01:07:01,783
Animal law humor.

1769
01:07:01,783 --> 01:07:05,016
- [Crow] But keep in mind,
I'm intensely stupid.

1770
01:07:05,016 --> 01:07:06,884
- Well we might as well get out of here.

1771
01:07:06,884 --> 01:07:10,148
- [Joel] It's TGIF, huh, fellas?

1772
01:07:10,148 --> 01:07:12,514
- [Tom] Hmong folk art.

1773
01:07:12,514 --> 01:07:15,933
- [Crow] We're worried about
your not smoking, Chad.

1774
01:07:15,933 --> 01:07:17,340
- You got something in mind, Steve?

1775
01:07:17,340 --> 01:07:18,997
- [Joel] Yeah, remember Bub's Daddy gum?

1776
01:07:18,997 --> 01:07:20,945
- I'm thinking of what
Evan said about the gators.

1777
01:07:20,945 --> 01:07:23,362
- [Tom] Yeah, he said, "Ahh!"

1778
01:07:25,479 --> 01:07:27,604
- Something made them leave, maybe a...

1779
01:07:27,604 --> 01:07:28,437
- [Joel] Court order?

1780
01:07:28,437 --> 01:07:30,135
- Mineral contamination.

1781
01:07:30,135 --> 01:07:31,640
- Guess we better run some water samples.

1782
01:07:31,640 --> 01:07:32,473
- [Crow] Through myself.

1783
01:07:32,473 --> 01:07:33,901
- Come on, you two, drink your coffee.

1784
01:07:33,901 --> 01:07:35,918
- [Tom] Well, I was just
talking about coffee.

1785
01:07:35,918 --> 01:07:38,429
- And supposing there
is no contamination...

1786
01:07:38,429 --> 01:07:39,610
- [Joel] Well we'll add some.

1787
01:07:39,610 --> 01:07:41,869
- Then I'll send over to
Eastpoint for a diving rig,

1788
01:07:41,869 --> 01:07:44,057
to go down to look for
this monster of yours.

1789
01:07:44,057 --> 01:07:45,920
- Now, Steve, don't even joke like that.

1790
01:07:45,920 --> 01:07:47,014
- [Crow] You don't know how to tell them.

1791
01:07:47,014 --> 01:07:47,847
- I'm not joking, Nan.

1792
01:07:47,847 --> 01:07:49,046
I've had some training with an aqualung

1793
01:07:49,046 --> 01:07:50,001
while I was in the Navy.

1794
01:07:50,001 --> 01:07:52,446
- [Tom] (mimics song
"Aqualung" by Jethro Tull)

1795
01:07:52,446 --> 01:07:55,049
Two bodies we know for sure
are down there, maybe four.

1796
01:07:55,049 --> 01:07:56,521
They've got to be brought up.

1797
01:07:56,521 --> 01:07:57,818
- Wait a minute.

1798
01:07:57,818 --> 01:08:00,510
There are no alligators in
that part of the swamp, right?

1799
01:08:00,510 --> 01:08:01,343
- [Steve] So?

1800
01:08:01,343 --> 01:08:03,354
- No sign of any fish or snakes?

1801
01:08:03,354 --> 01:08:04,271
- [Tom] No.

1802
01:08:05,229 --> 01:08:06,616
- Well, what are you getting at, Doc?

1803
01:08:06,616 --> 01:08:07,876
- [Crow] Uh, would you pass the jelly?

1804
01:08:07,876 --> 01:08:08,868
- Well there goes your argument about

1805
01:08:08,868 --> 01:08:11,199
setting off a dynamite charge underwater.

1806
01:08:11,199 --> 01:08:13,632
It'd bring the bodies to the surface.

1807
01:08:13,632 --> 01:08:14,609
- Get it out of your mind, Doc.

1808
01:08:14,609 --> 01:08:18,175
I'm not using any explosives
as long as there's another way.

1809
01:08:18,175 --> 01:08:19,255
- Why must you be such a...

1810
01:08:19,255 --> 01:08:20,088
- [Joel] Stud.

1811
01:08:20,088 --> 01:08:20,974
- Stubborn pig-head?

1812
01:08:20,974 --> 01:08:21,869
- [Nan] Exactly.

1813
01:08:21,869 --> 01:08:22,702
- [Tom] That, too.

1814
01:08:22,702 --> 01:08:23,998
- I'll tell you why.

1815
01:08:23,998 --> 01:08:25,664
Number one, there's bound
to be some aquarium life

1816
01:08:25,664 --> 01:08:28,457
in that section, even if the
bigger forms have abandoned it.

1817
01:08:28,457 --> 01:08:29,668
- [Joel] Get it?

1818
01:08:29,668 --> 01:08:32,056
- An explosion underwater would
destroy every living thing

1819
01:08:32,056 --> 01:08:34,114
and wreck what nature
took years to build up.

1820
01:08:34,114 --> 01:08:35,540
- [Tom] Years of inbreeding destroyed.

1821
01:08:35,540 --> 01:08:36,785
- Number two, I am paid to prevent

1822
01:08:36,785 --> 01:08:38,496
the useless slaughter of wildlife.

1823
01:08:38,496 --> 01:08:39,916
- [Crow] Uh, what was one again?

1824
01:08:39,916 --> 01:08:42,104
- Number three, I'd have
to get authorization,

1825
01:08:42,104 --> 01:08:43,060
and to get that authorization,

1826
01:08:43,060 --> 01:08:44,828
I'd have to come up with
a heck of a good reason.

1827
01:08:44,828 --> 01:08:46,451
- [Tom] Number four, you're a jerk.

1828
01:08:46,451 --> 01:08:47,910
- Satisfied?

1829
01:08:47,910 --> 01:08:49,716
- [Joel] No, I could use more coffee.

1830
01:08:49,716 --> 01:08:51,681
- Finish up, Dad, I wanna go home.

1831
01:08:51,681 --> 01:08:52,894
- [Tom] I thought you were home.

1832
01:08:52,894 --> 01:08:55,031
- Steve, how long would it take you

1833
01:08:55,031 --> 01:08:57,149
to get this diving
equipment from Eastpoint?

1834
01:08:57,149 --> 01:08:58,246
- [Crow] Is this a riddle?

1835
01:08:58,246 --> 01:08:59,996
- A couple days, why?

1836
01:09:00,846 --> 01:09:01,691
- Oh, nothing.

1837
01:09:01,691 --> 01:09:03,252
- [Joel] Then why bring it up?

1838
01:09:03,252 --> 01:09:05,668
- [Tom] I was deliberately
wasting your time.

1839
01:09:05,668 --> 01:09:07,334
- Take care of yourself, Steve.

1840
01:09:07,334 --> 01:09:10,537
- [Crow] Or someone might
stab you in your sleep.

1841
01:09:10,537 --> 01:09:11,600
- [Joel] Wait, I don't live here.

1842
01:09:11,600 --> 01:09:12,850
What the... oh.

1843
01:09:14,395 --> 01:09:15,879
- Well, here's hoping.

1844
01:09:15,879 --> 01:09:19,625
- Dad, can't you fill the
dynamite in from here?

1845
01:09:19,625 --> 01:09:22,414
- No, I wanna get it out in
the middle, the deepest spot.

1846
01:09:22,414 --> 01:09:25,311
- [Crow] 'Cause Pepperidge Farm remembers.

1847
01:09:25,311 --> 01:09:27,372
- [Tom] Now it's time for
Trout Blasting in America

1848
01:09:27,372 --> 01:09:28,908
with your host, Dick Branigan.

1849
01:09:28,908 --> 01:09:31,908
- [Joel] ♫ Oh, I'm a danger
to myself and others ♫

1850
01:09:31,908 --> 01:09:35,325
(mimics hillbilly music)

1851
01:09:37,055 --> 01:09:39,708
- [Crow] Well, goodbye
forever. I mean, Dad.

1852
01:09:39,708 --> 01:09:42,458
(dramatic music)

1853
01:09:44,651 --> 01:09:47,520
- [Tom] Ohh, I'm drained.

1854
01:09:47,520 --> 01:09:49,897
- [Joel] Our first sauna, big time.

1855
01:09:49,897 --> 01:09:52,147
(groaning)

1856
01:09:53,695 --> 01:09:56,496
- [Crow] Looks like the
cave of Dr. Calamari.

1857
01:09:56,496 --> 01:09:58,746
(groaning)

1858
01:09:59,923 --> 01:10:02,497
- [Joel] I know, I know,
you get used to it.

1859
01:10:02,497 --> 01:10:05,287
(dramatic music)

1860
01:10:05,287 --> 01:10:09,454
- [Tom] ♫ Offing an
alligator, living it up ♫

1861
01:10:12,003 --> 01:10:13,331
(wick burning)

1862
01:10:13,331 --> 01:10:16,216
- [Crow] Suck on that,
you darn son of a...

1863
01:10:16,216 --> 01:10:18,167
- [Joel] I pray I was adopted.

1864
01:10:18,167 --> 01:10:19,915
(birds chirping)

1865
01:10:19,915 --> 01:10:21,079
- [Tom] Well, honey, I
threw it under the boat

1866
01:10:21,079 --> 01:10:23,829
where it would do the most... ah!

1867
01:10:25,732 --> 01:10:28,440
- [Crow] Parking at the
swamp sure is convenient.

1868
01:10:28,440 --> 01:10:30,820
- [Joel] He's at it again, isn't he?

1869
01:10:30,820 --> 01:10:32,035
Sad, really.

1870
01:10:32,035 --> 01:10:34,618
- [Tom] Well, let's go get him.

1871
01:10:35,490 --> 01:10:37,823
(footsteps)

1872
01:10:39,279 --> 01:10:40,623
- [Crow] Come on, dummy.

1873
01:10:40,623 --> 01:10:42,790
Back to the home with you.

1874
01:10:44,352 --> 01:10:49,091
- [Tom] Now she's wearing
her beauty operator outfit.

1875
01:10:49,091 --> 01:10:50,356
- It should go any minute.

1876
01:10:50,356 --> 01:10:51,657
- [Joel] Yeah, right.

1877
01:10:51,657 --> 01:10:53,460
- [Crow] What a loon.

1878
01:10:53,460 --> 01:10:55,134
I'm not a loon.

1879
01:10:55,134 --> 01:10:56,131
(explosion)

1880
01:10:56,131 --> 01:10:58,422
- [Tom] Next week on MacGyver.

1881
01:10:58,422 --> 01:11:01,172
- [Joel] Everybody into the pool!

1882
01:11:02,108 --> 01:11:05,971
- [Crow] Well gee, it worked
on the gophers in my yard.

1883
01:11:05,971 --> 01:11:08,875
(foreboding music)

1884
01:11:08,875 --> 01:11:10,343
- [Joel] Huh?

1885
01:11:10,343 --> 01:11:12,926
(music swells)

1886
01:11:20,132 --> 01:11:21,925
- [Tom] That was Bob there!

1887
01:11:21,925 --> 01:11:22,925
Get it, Bob?

1888
01:11:24,122 --> 01:11:24,955
Sorry.

1889
01:11:26,961 --> 01:11:27,794
- [Crow] Well, let me tell you,

1890
01:11:27,794 --> 01:11:29,865
if I can take one dumb
thing back in my life...

1891
01:11:29,865 --> 01:11:31,543
- [Tom] I know what you mean.

1892
01:11:31,543 --> 01:11:34,876
- [Joel] Say, are those Bugle Boy jeans?

1893
01:11:38,321 --> 01:11:42,404
- [Tom] (yawn) I've seen better.

1894
01:11:51,394 --> 01:11:53,033
- Bring it down there, Gypsy.

1895
01:11:53,033 --> 01:11:56,436
You know, Silas, it's not
easy being a social misfit

1896
01:11:56,436 --> 01:11:58,670
and then getting the added responsibility

1897
01:11:58,670 --> 01:12:01,686
of dragging out in the
swamp for missing townsfolk,

1898
01:12:01,686 --> 01:12:03,696
and I can't even button my own shirt.

1899
01:12:03,696 --> 01:12:05,250
- [Tom] I hear ya, Gunther.

1900
01:12:05,250 --> 01:12:07,758
Isn't it amazing how we inferior types

1901
01:12:07,758 --> 01:12:09,776
keep getting asked to
do the dangerous work

1902
01:12:09,776 --> 01:12:12,227
that should go to men for stable than us?

1903
01:12:12,227 --> 01:12:14,026
Really is a miracle, hee hoo!

1904
01:12:14,026 --> 01:12:14,859
Hunter?

1905
01:12:14,859 --> 01:12:17,526
- Duh, yup, uh huh, wag nuts.

1906
01:12:17,526 --> 01:12:18,916
- What'd he say, Silas?

1907
01:12:18,916 --> 01:12:21,441
- I think what our bright young
friend's trying to say here

1908
01:12:21,441 --> 01:12:23,889
is the reason we three goofuses are asked

1909
01:12:23,889 --> 01:12:26,256
to do these hazardous tasks
outside the perimeters

1910
01:12:26,256 --> 01:12:28,472
of normal society's rationale is

1911
01:12:28,472 --> 01:12:30,762
we're a danger to ourselves and others.

1912
01:12:30,762 --> 01:12:32,426
- Aw, kinda reminds me of

1913
01:12:32,426 --> 01:12:34,543
Darwin's theory of natural selection.

1914
01:12:34,543 --> 01:12:36,727
- Yeah, if you're dumb, you die!

1915
01:12:36,727 --> 01:12:38,125
(laughter)

1916
01:12:38,125 --> 01:12:40,958
- That's a rich one there, Hunter.

1917
01:12:42,360 --> 01:12:44,503
- ♫ Oh, I'm a danger to myself and others

1918
01:12:44,503 --> 01:12:46,339
♫ My cousins are as close as brothers

1919
01:12:46,339 --> 01:12:49,407
♫ I stay out in the rain all the time

1920
01:12:49,407 --> 01:12:51,656
- ♫ He's a danger to himself and others

1921
01:12:51,656 --> 01:12:53,643
♫ Kinda likes shows with Sally Struthers

1922
01:12:53,643 --> 01:12:57,126
♫ I can't even think of a word that rhymes

1923
01:12:57,126 --> 01:12:58,723
♫ How dumb are you, Uncle Dad

1924
01:12:58,723 --> 01:13:00,641
♫ Well, pretty dumb, that's for sure

1925
01:13:00,641 --> 01:13:02,215
♫ How dumb are you, Uncle Dad

1926
01:13:02,215 --> 01:13:04,166
♫ Well this pot's filled with manure

1927
01:13:04,166 --> 01:13:06,076
♫ We're a danger to ourselves and others

1928
01:13:06,076 --> 01:13:07,927
♫ Save the Earth and steal our mothers

1929
01:13:07,927 --> 01:13:11,060
♫ Leave us in the woods
and we're just fine

1930
01:13:11,060 --> 01:13:13,274
♫ We're a danger to ourselves and others

1931
01:13:13,274 --> 01:13:15,211
♫ Good luck stuck with better lovers

1932
01:13:15,211 --> 01:13:19,114
♫ Hunting leech is what
we call a good time ♫

1933
01:13:19,114 --> 01:13:21,747
(explosion)

1934
01:13:21,747 --> 01:13:23,024
- Boy, oh boy!

1935
01:13:23,024 --> 01:13:24,238
I'd like to shake hands with any

1936
01:13:24,238 --> 01:13:25,674
giant leech that lived through that!

1937
01:13:25,674 --> 01:13:27,894
- What, the dynamite or that crappy song?

1938
01:13:27,894 --> 01:13:30,143
(banjo music)

1939
01:13:30,143 --> 01:13:34,310
(Mystery Science Theater 3000 theme)

1940
01:13:36,783 --> 01:13:40,440
(mimicking banjo music)

1941
01:13:40,440 --> 01:13:41,583
- [Tom] Good stuff.

1942
01:13:41,583 --> 01:13:45,131
- Walker's wife, why didn't
her body come to the surface?

1943
01:13:45,131 --> 01:13:46,149
- I don't know.

1944
01:13:46,149 --> 01:13:48,799
- [Crow] Maybe her shoes were too heavy.

1945
01:13:48,799 --> 01:13:51,910
- I think I better walk over
to the coroner's office.

1946
01:13:51,910 --> 01:13:53,951
- What's the matter, Mr. Benton?

1947
01:13:53,951 --> 01:13:56,449
Feel awkward about arresting
my father in his own home?

1948
01:13:56,449 --> 01:13:57,282
- [Tom] No.

1949
01:13:57,282 --> 01:13:59,717
- I warned him I'd arrest
him if he used dynamite.

1950
01:13:59,717 --> 01:14:01,218
- That was nice of you.

1951
01:14:01,218 --> 01:14:03,470
- [Joel] Geez, I don't get women.

1952
01:14:03,470 --> 01:14:05,848
- Well, the autopsy should be over by now.

1953
01:14:05,848 --> 01:14:07,419
(door opens)

1954
01:14:07,419 --> 01:14:10,099
- [Tom] Oh, hi, you got the
right to remain silent, and...

1955
01:14:10,099 --> 01:14:11,631
- I thought you'd be here.

1956
01:14:11,631 --> 01:14:12,883
- [Crow] I was wishing you weren't.

1957
01:14:12,883 --> 01:14:15,314
- I don't like to have to do this, Doctor.

1958
01:14:15,314 --> 01:14:17,650
- Do we have time for
a cup of coffee first?

1959
01:14:17,650 --> 01:14:18,900
- Sure.

1960
01:14:18,900 --> 01:14:20,294
- Fine, honey?

1961
01:14:20,294 --> 01:14:21,560
- No, I'll make some sandwiches.

1962
01:14:21,560 --> 01:14:23,737
- [Joel] I'll make some coffee sandwiches.

1963
01:14:23,737 --> 01:14:27,904
- [Crow] She's going to his
place to make sandwiches.

1964
01:14:29,516 --> 01:14:31,847
- [Tom] Ahh, you know liquor
makes everything right.

1965
01:14:31,847 --> 01:14:32,864
- [Joel] From the liquor council.

1966
01:14:32,864 --> 01:14:34,481
- [Crow] It's what your
body's thirsty for.

1967
01:14:34,481 --> 01:14:35,579
- All the good it'll do now.

1968
01:14:35,579 --> 01:14:37,037
- [Crow] Let me show you my appendix scar.

1969
01:14:37,037 --> 01:14:40,091
- Dave Walker didn't shoot Cal.

1970
01:14:40,091 --> 01:14:42,591
- [Joel] I'll get more coffee.

1971
01:14:43,706 --> 01:14:45,780
- Kovis tried to keep my out,

1972
01:14:45,780 --> 01:14:47,081
but I told him I'd raise a stink

1973
01:14:47,081 --> 01:14:48,992
they'd smell all the way to the capitol.

1974
01:14:48,992 --> 01:14:50,387
- [Tom] Don't doubt that, heh.

1975
01:14:50,387 --> 01:14:51,851
- What killed them, Doc?

1976
01:14:51,851 --> 01:14:53,654
- Not drowning.

1977
01:14:53,654 --> 01:14:55,138
Cal wasn't shot, either.

1978
01:14:55,138 --> 01:14:57,023
- [Crow] And he wasn't beat up by clowns.

1979
01:14:57,023 --> 01:14:59,387
- Every drop of blood was
drained out of their bodies.

1980
01:14:59,387 --> 01:15:00,284
- [Joel] Turns out I did it.

1981
01:15:00,284 --> 01:15:01,117
Funny, huh?

1982
01:15:01,117 --> 01:15:05,819
- And they had wounds on
their throats, suction wounds.

1983
01:15:05,819 --> 01:15:07,211
Like a gigantic leech bite.

1984
01:15:07,211 --> 01:15:08,680
- [Tom] Or a Hoover.

1985
01:15:08,680 --> 01:15:10,406
- And there's something
even more incredible.

1986
01:15:10,406 --> 01:15:11,574
- [Crow] I'm Skippy the chimp.

1987
01:15:11,574 --> 01:15:14,457
- Cal was supposed to have
been killed several days ago.

1988
01:15:14,457 --> 01:15:18,193
Sam and Reed had been
missing a matter of 48 hours.

1989
01:15:18,193 --> 01:15:19,631
All three of them, as far as we know,

1990
01:15:19,631 --> 01:15:22,991
were in that lake a minimum of two days.

1991
01:15:22,991 --> 01:15:25,310
Now I'd stake my reputation as a doctor...

1992
01:15:25,310 --> 01:15:26,143
- [Tom] Or what's left of it.

1993
01:15:26,143 --> 01:15:27,095
- Cal hadn't been dead more than

1994
01:15:27,095 --> 01:15:29,556
two or three hours before we found him.

1995
01:15:29,556 --> 01:15:31,566
The other two, less than that.

1996
01:15:31,566 --> 01:15:34,508
First stage rigor mortis
started in during the autopsy.

1997
01:15:34,508 --> 01:15:35,896
- But how could that be, Doc?

1998
01:15:35,896 --> 01:15:37,284
It's impossible.

1999
01:15:37,284 --> 01:15:39,348
- The coroner will confirm it.

2000
01:15:39,348 --> 01:15:40,416
- [Joel] Cigarette?

2001
01:15:40,416 --> 01:15:42,082
- Whatever killed them
is still in that lake,

2002
01:15:42,082 --> 01:15:44,912
and it's gonna take more
than dynamite to get it out.

2003
01:15:44,912 --> 01:15:47,452
- [Crow] I think we
should use my new A-bomb!

2004
01:15:47,452 --> 01:15:48,394
- The concussion from those charges

2005
01:15:48,394 --> 01:15:49,973
would've killed a full-grown whale.

2006
01:15:49,973 --> 01:15:53,133
- [Tom] That's it, we'll put
a full-grown whale down there.

2007
01:15:53,133 --> 01:15:54,730
- That explains how Cal
and and the others lived

2008
01:15:54,730 --> 01:15:57,059
after they were supposed to be drowned.

2009
01:15:57,059 --> 01:15:58,587
- What do you mean?

2010
01:15:58,587 --> 01:16:01,156
- This whole region is
riddled by caves and caverns

2011
01:16:01,156 --> 01:16:03,544
cut out by the ocean
thousands of years ago.

2012
01:16:03,544 --> 01:16:04,529
- [Joel] I love riddles.

2013
01:16:04,529 --> 01:16:05,609
- If there are caves above the water,

2014
01:16:05,609 --> 01:16:07,823
then there must be caves under the water.

2015
01:16:07,823 --> 01:16:08,656
- Go on.

2016
01:16:08,656 --> 01:16:10,144
- [Tom] No, make me.

2017
01:16:10,144 --> 01:16:11,522
- Mike and I had a chance to fool around

2018
01:16:11,522 --> 01:16:14,280
with some frogman equipment
captured from the Italian Navy.

2019
01:16:14,280 --> 01:16:16,435
- [Crow] And then we went
on to showgirl costumes.

2020
01:16:16,435 --> 01:16:18,723
- Prowled through a sunken
transport off Salerno.

2021
01:16:18,723 --> 01:16:20,204
- [Joel] Butter cookie?

2022
01:16:20,204 --> 01:16:21,037
- When the ship went down,

2023
01:16:21,037 --> 01:16:23,725
it trapped some air inside the hull.

2024
01:16:23,725 --> 01:16:26,665
We'd go down, come up
inside the officer's lounge,

2025
01:16:26,665 --> 01:16:28,973
take off our face masks, and sorta sample

2026
01:16:28,973 --> 01:16:30,455
some of the bottles floating around.

2027
01:16:30,455 --> 01:16:32,108
- [Tom] That's nice, is there
a point to your little story?

2028
01:16:32,108 --> 01:16:33,239
- It drove the guys crazy
trying to figure out

2029
01:16:33,239 --> 01:16:35,823
how we were getting the stuff.

2030
01:16:35,823 --> 01:16:38,049
- You mean you think that
there's an air pocket

2031
01:16:38,049 --> 01:16:39,868
or a cave underneath the lake?

2032
01:16:39,868 --> 01:16:40,989
- Right, gotta be.

2033
01:16:40,989 --> 01:16:43,054
How else could those people
have lived down there?

2034
01:16:43,054 --> 01:16:44,615
Not only that, it explains how

2035
01:16:44,615 --> 01:16:46,880
whatever it is down there
lived through the concussions.

2036
01:16:46,880 --> 01:16:47,713
- [Doc] Steve!

2037
01:16:47,713 --> 01:16:48,696
- [Tom] Steve!

2038
01:16:48,696 --> 01:16:49,979
Spock!

2039
01:16:49,979 --> 01:16:51,047
- Liz Walker.

2040
01:16:51,047 --> 01:16:52,727
- [Crow] I stood up too fast. Whoo!

2041
01:16:52,727 --> 01:16:56,503
- She might still be down there, alive.

2042
01:16:56,503 --> 01:16:58,086
- [Joel] But musty.

2043
01:16:59,711 --> 01:17:01,095
- [Crow] What?

2044
01:17:01,095 --> 01:17:03,515
(eerie music)

2045
01:17:03,515 --> 01:17:04,870
(groaning)

2046
01:17:04,870 --> 01:17:07,050
- [Tom] Calgon, take me away.

2047
01:17:07,050 --> 01:17:09,050
- [Crow] I think it did.

2048
01:17:10,331 --> 01:17:14,766
This must be the swimsuit
edition of Leech Illustrated.

2049
01:17:14,766 --> 01:17:16,335
- [Joel] A lick of paint,
some nice curtains,

2050
01:17:16,335 --> 01:17:18,391
this place would be okay.

2051
01:17:18,391 --> 01:17:22,587
- [Tom] Meanwhile, on Hollywood Boulevard.

2052
01:17:22,587 --> 01:17:25,788
- [Crow] We now return to Hawaii Five-0.

2053
01:17:25,788 --> 01:17:28,556
- [Joel] Looks like the
cameraman can't find the cast.

2054
01:17:28,556 --> 01:17:31,306
(dramatic music)

2055
01:17:33,147 --> 01:17:34,564
- [Crow] Skipper.

2056
01:17:37,070 --> 01:17:40,570
Now you bring the swamp to a rolling boil.

2057
01:17:41,418 --> 01:17:43,770
- [Joel] Hey, it looks
like the dad from Flipper.

2058
01:17:43,770 --> 01:17:46,520
(dolphin noises)

2059
01:17:47,762 --> 01:17:49,603
- [Tom] Hey, looks like the whole town

2060
01:17:49,603 --> 01:17:51,186
came out to watch them suit up.

2061
01:17:51,186 --> 01:17:52,646
- [Crow] Well, they
don't have a movie house.

2062
01:17:52,646 --> 01:17:54,478
- [Tom] Yup.

2063
01:17:54,478 --> 01:17:55,342
- [Steve] Comfortable?

2064
01:17:55,342 --> 01:17:56,175
- Yeah.

2065
01:17:56,175 --> 01:17:57,191
- [Joel] Make a nice living.

2066
01:17:57,191 --> 01:17:59,002
- You sure you don't want
me to go down for you, Mike?

2067
01:17:59,002 --> 01:18:01,098
You haven't had one of these
things on for a long time,

2068
01:18:01,098 --> 01:18:03,249
while me, every other day
I've been playing fish.

2069
01:18:03,249 --> 01:18:06,822
- [Tom] Remember the first
time we had one of these on?

2070
01:18:06,822 --> 01:18:08,429
(silence)

2071
01:18:08,429 --> 01:18:11,207
- If you ask me, they ought
to have their heads examined.

2072
01:18:11,207 --> 01:18:12,409
Giant leeches.

2073
01:18:12,409 --> 01:18:14,543
- [All] (groans)

2074
01:18:14,543 --> 01:18:15,775
- I'm willing to bet a month's pay

2075
01:18:15,775 --> 01:18:18,278
they don't come up with
nothing more than excuses.

2076
01:18:18,278 --> 01:18:20,194
- [Crow] Well I could use 10 bucks.

2077
01:18:20,194 --> 01:18:23,050
- Him, the doc, and his brat, too.

2078
01:18:23,050 --> 01:18:25,974
Just wait 'til I get on
the phone with the capitol.

2079
01:18:25,974 --> 01:18:27,227
I'll guarantee somebody will wish

2080
01:18:27,227 --> 01:18:29,924
they'd kept their nose out
of other people's business.

2081
01:18:29,924 --> 01:18:33,257
- [Joel] And out of my underwear drawer.

2082
01:18:34,560 --> 01:18:37,310
- [All] (groans)

2083
01:18:40,392 --> 01:18:41,557
- [Nan] Steve!

2084
01:18:41,557 --> 01:18:43,088
- [Tom] I'm going with you.

2085
01:18:43,088 --> 01:18:44,890
- Be careful, please.

2086
01:18:44,890 --> 01:18:46,793
- Don't worry, Mike will be
ready if anything goes wrong.

2087
01:18:46,793 --> 01:18:48,360
- Don't worry about Steve.

2088
01:18:48,360 --> 01:18:49,810
Now if he were going after barracuda,

2089
01:18:49,810 --> 01:18:51,116
you might have a tiny bit to worry about.

2090
01:18:51,116 --> 01:18:55,283
- [All] (mimic the music
of "Barracuda" by Heart)

2091
01:18:57,159 --> 01:19:00,505
- [Crow] Sure you don't want some coffee?

2092
01:19:00,505 --> 01:19:03,046
(dramatic music)

2093
01:19:03,046 --> 01:19:05,336
- [Joel] Goodbye,
Calgon. Goodbye, Skipper.

2094
01:19:05,336 --> 01:19:08,919
Sure hope those coconut
air tanks hold out.

2095
01:19:13,946 --> 01:19:15,369
- [Tom] They're dead. Goodbye!

2096
01:19:15,369 --> 01:19:16,451
They don't have a chance.

2097
01:19:16,451 --> 01:19:17,789
I'll see you later, good luck!

2098
01:19:17,789 --> 01:19:18,622
We'll drag them out tomorrow.

2099
01:19:18,622 --> 01:19:20,404
Good luck out there!

2100
01:19:20,404 --> 01:19:21,989
- [Crow] It's the star of Ralph Bellamy,

2101
01:19:21,989 --> 01:19:24,201
Ralph Bellamy, Ralph Bellamy.

2102
01:19:24,201 --> 01:19:27,539
(suspenseful music)

2103
01:19:27,539 --> 01:19:29,275
- [Joel] I see London, I see France,

2104
01:19:29,275 --> 01:19:32,801
I see little Steve-O's underpants.

2105
01:19:32,801 --> 01:19:34,868
(low growling)

2106
01:19:34,868 --> 01:19:36,674
- [Crow] Good news,
we're having fish today.

2107
01:19:36,674 --> 01:19:38,591
Heh heh, fish... sorry.

2108
01:19:40,151 --> 01:19:41,532
- No, no, please stay away.

2109
01:19:41,532 --> 01:19:42,771
- [Joel] No, you suck the life out of me.

2110
01:19:42,771 --> 01:19:44,618
I have nothing more to give.

2111
01:19:44,618 --> 01:19:45,451
- Please.

2112
01:19:47,319 --> 01:19:49,926
- Not exactly inviting, is it?

2113
01:19:49,926 --> 01:19:51,133
- Nah, it sure isn't.

2114
01:19:51,133 --> 01:19:52,185
- [Tom] Hey, let's just stay in the boat

2115
01:19:52,185 --> 01:19:54,896
and just eat sandwiches, okay?

2116
01:19:54,896 --> 01:19:59,487
- [Crow] And look, I'll spit
in my own mask this time.

2117
01:19:59,487 --> 01:20:00,320
(splash)

2118
01:20:00,320 --> 01:20:01,264
- [Joel] Right to the bottom.

2119
01:20:01,264 --> 01:20:03,393
Well, let's try the other one.

2120
01:20:03,393 --> 01:20:04,975
- [Tom] Geez, that's not deep.

2121
01:20:04,975 --> 01:20:09,074
It was filmed in Corman's swimming pool.

2122
01:20:09,074 --> 01:20:11,074
- [Joel] Darn fool kids.

2123
01:20:13,181 --> 01:20:14,548
- [Tom] Watch that end.

2124
01:20:14,548 --> 01:20:15,381
Oh, sorry.

2125
01:20:15,381 --> 01:20:17,385
- It'll stop anything up to a tiger shark.

2126
01:20:17,385 --> 01:20:19,802
But you gotta hit, misses don't count.

2127
01:20:19,802 --> 01:20:22,014
- Anything goes wrong,
just yank on the line

2128
01:20:22,014 --> 01:20:23,103
and I'll be down in a hurry.

2129
01:20:23,103 --> 01:20:27,052
- [Crow] And yank real hard,
I might have the radio on.

2130
01:20:27,052 --> 01:20:30,571
(dramatic music)

2131
01:20:30,571 --> 01:20:33,238
- [All] (groan)

2132
01:20:35,847 --> 01:20:40,014
(Mystery Science Theater 3000 theme)

2133
01:20:40,912 --> 01:20:43,782
(bubbles)

2134
01:20:43,782 --> 01:20:46,719
- [Tom] ♫ I'd like to be under the sea ♫

2135
01:20:46,719 --> 01:20:48,332
- [Joel] Huh, what's this?

2136
01:20:48,332 --> 01:20:50,898
It's a little mailbox
that reads, "I am Leech."

2137
01:20:50,898 --> 01:20:53,231
This must be his place, huh?

2138
01:20:55,155 --> 01:20:56,887
- [Crow] What a jerk.

2139
01:20:56,887 --> 01:20:59,637
(dramatic music)

2140
01:21:03,078 --> 01:21:05,745
(leech roaring)

2141
01:21:08,749 --> 01:21:09,779
(yanking)

2142
01:21:09,779 --> 01:21:11,694
- [Joel] Yank, yank, yank!

2143
01:21:11,694 --> 01:21:13,456
Hello, is this thing on?

2144
01:21:13,456 --> 01:21:15,007
Big thing!

2145
01:21:15,007 --> 01:21:17,757
(dramatic music)

2146
01:21:20,809 --> 01:21:24,976
- [Tom] Uppies! I want uppies! (cries)

2147
01:21:26,890 --> 01:21:27,816
Oh, got a big one.

2148
01:21:27,816 --> 01:21:31,884
- [Crow] (gasps) Could
you hand me another spear?

2149
01:21:31,884 --> 01:21:32,718
- Sure you don't want me to go down

2150
01:21:32,718 --> 01:21:34,369
and finish it off for you, Steve?

2151
01:21:34,369 --> 01:21:35,993
- No, thanks, Mike.

2152
01:21:35,993 --> 01:21:38,825
I think I hurt it pretty bad.

2153
01:21:38,825 --> 01:21:39,658
- Careful now.

2154
01:21:39,658 --> 01:21:42,761
Anything that hurts is
ten times as dangerous.

2155
01:21:42,761 --> 01:21:46,693
- [Joel] Yeah, I'll figure
that out underwater, friend.

2156
01:21:46,693 --> 01:21:48,147
- He's wounded it!

2157
01:21:48,147 --> 01:21:49,529
He's gonna finish it off!

2158
01:21:49,529 --> 01:21:51,115
- [Tom] I'd start the grill if I were you!

2159
01:21:51,115 --> 01:21:53,013
- Does he have to go under again?

2160
01:21:53,013 --> 01:21:54,929
- He's got to make sure, honey.

2161
01:21:54,929 --> 01:21:56,619
- [Crow] This is what therapy's all about.

2162
01:21:56,619 --> 01:21:58,763
- Where could it have come from?

2163
01:21:58,763 --> 01:22:00,493
- I wish I knew.

2164
01:22:00,493 --> 01:22:02,230
We'll have to make some tests.

2165
01:22:02,230 --> 01:22:03,063
- [Tom] Like SAT?

2166
01:22:03,063 --> 01:22:04,448
- Maybe the proximity of Cape Canaveral's

2167
01:22:04,448 --> 01:22:06,164
got something to do with it.

2168
01:22:06,164 --> 01:22:07,585
- The rocket station?

2169
01:22:07,585 --> 01:22:08,716
- Well, they use atomic energy

2170
01:22:08,716 --> 01:22:10,619
in their first stages of launching.

2171
01:22:10,619 --> 01:22:13,164
Not all of them have been successful.

2172
01:22:13,164 --> 01:22:15,507
- You think innocent
animal life was close by.

2173
01:22:15,507 --> 01:22:16,643
Not close enough to be killed,

2174
01:22:16,643 --> 01:22:18,588
but close enough to feel the effects

2175
01:22:18,588 --> 01:22:20,110
of a radioactive energy that--

2176
01:22:20,110 --> 01:22:21,142
- A mutation.

2177
01:22:21,142 --> 01:22:24,359
A type of gigantism of some common animal.

2178
01:22:24,359 --> 01:22:25,192
- [Joel] (snoring)

2179
01:22:25,192 --> 01:22:26,234
- [Crow] Speaking of common animals,

2180
01:22:26,234 --> 01:22:27,792
how in the world is Steve getting on?

2181
01:22:27,792 --> 01:22:29,915
- I hope he's able to bring it up.

2182
01:22:29,915 --> 01:22:32,841
- [Tom] Heh, then I can bring my lunch up.

2183
01:22:32,841 --> 01:22:36,828
- [Joel] Let's go see what
Steve the jerk is up to.

2184
01:22:36,828 --> 01:22:38,224
- [Crow] Hey, I can't see.

2185
01:22:38,224 --> 01:22:40,974
(dramatic music)

2186
01:22:43,206 --> 01:22:45,539
(splashing)

2187
01:22:47,678 --> 01:22:50,144
- [Joel] I feel like I'm in Hef's grotto.

2188
01:22:50,144 --> 01:22:52,394
(groaning)

2189
01:22:54,696 --> 01:22:55,860
- [Tom] Whoop, there she goes.

2190
01:22:55,860 --> 01:22:58,290
Buh-bye now, heh, buh-bye.

2191
01:22:58,290 --> 01:23:01,040
(dramatic music)

2192
01:23:03,173 --> 01:23:06,689
- [Crow] Oh, I've sen this in Dementia 13.

2193
01:23:06,689 --> 01:23:09,657
(music swells)

2194
01:23:09,657 --> 01:23:11,009
- [Tom] I see London, I see France--

2195
01:23:11,009 --> 01:23:13,517
- [Joel] Hey, come on, she's dead.

2196
01:23:13,517 --> 01:23:15,221
- [Crow] Huh?

2197
01:23:15,221 --> 01:23:17,404
(music swells)

2198
01:23:17,404 --> 01:23:18,275
- [Tom] Hey, good job!

2199
01:23:18,275 --> 01:23:19,525
They found her.

2200
01:23:21,225 --> 01:23:23,975
(water sloshing)

2201
01:23:26,356 --> 01:23:29,428
- [Tom] I'll just put her on the stringer.

2202
01:23:29,428 --> 01:23:31,095
- That can't be Liz.

2203
01:23:32,669 --> 01:23:34,567
- [Joel] I don't remember Liz being dead

2204
01:23:34,567 --> 01:23:36,417
with giant hickeys on her cheeks.

2205
01:23:36,417 --> 01:23:37,995
- What could've done that?

2206
01:23:37,995 --> 01:23:38,828
- [Crow] Steven Tyler?

2207
01:23:38,828 --> 01:23:40,411
- Look at her face.

2208
01:23:41,718 --> 01:23:42,917
- [Tom] Maybe.

2209
01:23:42,917 --> 01:23:45,370
(bubbles)

2210
01:23:45,370 --> 01:23:46,850
- [Tom] The abyss.

2211
01:23:46,850 --> 01:23:49,901
- [Joel] Owie, I just got
such a big blood blister

2212
01:23:49,901 --> 01:23:54,035
and it's so nice and juicy
and I feel so naughty.

2213
01:23:54,035 --> 01:23:56,944
Oh, I sure hope a big
leech doesn't come around.

2214
01:23:56,944 --> 01:23:59,082
It would be just terrible.

2215
01:23:59,082 --> 01:24:00,082
- [Crow] Sucker!

2216
01:24:00,082 --> 01:24:03,040
Wait a minute, that's me. (laughs)

2217
01:24:03,040 --> 01:24:05,873
(dramatic music)

2218
01:24:07,178 --> 01:24:10,585
- [Tom] Now it looks like a sea turtle.

2219
01:24:10,585 --> 01:24:13,418
(leech roaring)

2220
01:24:13,418 --> 01:24:16,126
(music swells)

2221
01:24:16,126 --> 01:24:19,141
- [Tom] Uh, think Liz will be
okay in that boat all alone?

2222
01:24:19,141 --> 01:24:21,572
- [Crow] Steve was in
trouble, I had to act fast.

2223
01:24:21,572 --> 01:24:24,218
I had to get my sons in the series.

2224
01:24:24,218 --> 01:24:26,885
(leech roaring)

2225
01:24:28,167 --> 01:24:32,572
- [Tom] Hey Ma, I'd like to
borrow your butcher knife, okay?

2226
01:24:32,572 --> 01:24:34,335
- [Joel] Wait a minute,
are there two of them now?

2227
01:24:34,335 --> 01:24:35,807
I don't get what's going on.

2228
01:24:35,807 --> 01:24:36,752
- [Crow] Oh, it's a mess.

2229
01:24:36,752 --> 01:24:38,186
- [Tom] It's confusing.

2230
01:24:38,186 --> 01:24:39,853
Doesn't matter, huh?

2231
01:24:41,301 --> 01:24:43,218
Don't touch him, that's my pal!

2232
01:24:43,218 --> 01:24:44,470
- [Crow] He's trying
to stab the camera man.

2233
01:24:44,470 --> 01:24:46,237
Did you see that?

2234
01:24:46,237 --> 01:24:47,470
(bubbles)

2235
01:24:47,470 --> 01:24:48,620
- [Tom] Not me, you idiot.

2236
01:24:48,620 --> 01:24:50,370
The leech, the leech!

2237
01:24:51,352 --> 01:24:52,481
- [Joel] So there's two of you, huh?

2238
01:24:52,481 --> 01:24:54,017
Come on, come on, I've
sucked blood from guys

2239
01:24:54,017 --> 01:24:56,911
bigger than the both of you.

2240
01:24:56,911 --> 01:24:58,393
- [Tom] Then it was back to the lodge

2241
01:24:58,393 --> 01:25:00,692
for a smooth Canadian Club.

2242
01:25:00,692 --> 01:25:03,573
(dramatic music)

2243
01:25:03,573 --> 01:25:05,657
- [Crow] Oh, it's a manatee.

2244
01:25:05,657 --> 01:25:08,462
- [All] (groans)

2245
01:25:08,462 --> 01:25:09,325
- I've been around here for years.

2246
01:25:09,325 --> 01:25:10,727
I never saw nothing like that.

2247
01:25:10,727 --> 01:25:13,727
- [Crow] I'm a professional skeptic.

2248
01:25:15,044 --> 01:25:19,211
- [Joel] Tommy Bartlett invites
you to ride the wild leech.

2249
01:25:20,105 --> 01:25:22,900
- [Tom] Come on, let's all ride him!

2250
01:25:22,900 --> 01:25:24,733
- [Crow] David Jensen?

2251
01:25:26,401 --> 01:25:27,835
- [Tom] Might wanna
measure her and weight her.

2252
01:25:27,835 --> 01:25:28,668
I think she's a keeper.

2253
01:25:28,668 --> 01:25:30,668
She's a record, alright.

2254
01:25:34,054 --> 01:25:35,933
- [Joel] Whoop, there goes Liz.

2255
01:25:35,933 --> 01:25:38,683
(water sloshing)

2256
01:25:41,586 --> 01:25:45,685
- [Tom] Hey, I'll have
to go diving more often.

2257
01:25:45,685 --> 01:25:46,554
I'll carry her.

2258
01:25:46,554 --> 01:25:47,387
- [Crow] No, I'll carry her.

2259
01:25:47,387 --> 01:25:48,220
- [Tom] I'll carry here.

2260
01:25:48,220 --> 01:25:50,420
- [Joel] I'll get her.

2261
01:25:50,420 --> 01:25:53,556
- [Tom] And what of Emily
and her love for Chad?

2262
01:25:53,556 --> 01:25:56,006
And what of the hair on Chuck's chest?

2263
01:25:56,006 --> 01:25:56,923
Stay tuned.

2264
01:25:58,555 --> 01:26:00,055
- [Joel] Epilogue.

2265
01:26:02,053 --> 01:26:04,803
(birds chirping)

2266
01:26:06,786 --> 01:26:09,965
- [Crow] Who's this guy,
a roadie from Sea Hunt?

2267
01:26:09,965 --> 01:26:13,937
- [Joel] Wish I would've thought of that.

2268
01:26:13,937 --> 01:26:16,314
(whistles)

2269
01:26:16,314 --> 01:26:17,981
- [Tom] Anytime now!

2270
01:26:18,830 --> 01:26:20,617
- They must be licking their wounds.

2271
01:26:20,617 --> 01:26:21,828
(slurping sounds)

2272
01:26:21,828 --> 01:26:23,706
- Sure you used enough
stuff to do the job, Mike?

2273
01:26:23,706 --> 01:26:25,451
- I used 100 sticks of 40%.

2274
01:26:25,451 --> 01:26:28,000
- [Joel] Enough to kill all of us.

2275
01:26:28,000 --> 01:26:29,403
- Alright, let her go.

2276
01:26:29,403 --> 01:26:32,392
- Oh, no, I've done
enough of your dirty work.

2277
01:26:32,392 --> 01:26:33,559
Help yourself.

2278
01:26:34,496 --> 01:26:36,784
- [Tom] No, I'm not
falling for that old one.

2279
01:26:36,784 --> 01:26:38,758
- [Joel] Let's see, how do
you work this old thing?

2280
01:26:38,758 --> 01:26:41,769
Let's see if my memory
serves me correctly.

2281
01:26:41,769 --> 01:26:43,418
(explosion)

2282
01:26:43,418 --> 01:26:45,671
- [Joel] Blew my blanket clean off!

2283
01:26:45,671 --> 01:26:48,636
(foreboding music)

2284
01:26:48,636 --> 01:26:49,636
- [All] One.

2285
01:26:52,323 --> 01:26:54,666
- [Tom] Well I'll be
hogtied in the backroom

2286
01:26:54,666 --> 01:26:56,083
with a big old...

2287
01:26:57,313 --> 01:26:59,067
- [Joel] Fish fry tonight.

2288
01:26:59,067 --> 01:27:00,067
- [All] Two.

2289
01:27:02,632 --> 01:27:05,002
- [Tom] I'd hoped that'd be more exciting.

2290
01:27:05,002 --> 01:27:06,381
Well let's go back to town...

2291
01:27:06,381 --> 01:27:07,783
Oh wait, there is no town.

2292
01:27:07,783 --> 01:27:09,361
- [Joel] Now who's
hungry for blood sausage?

2293
01:27:09,361 --> 01:27:13,281
Just kidding! (laughs)

2294
01:27:13,281 --> 01:27:14,582
- [Crow] Well I'm gonna miss him.

2295
01:27:14,582 --> 01:27:18,290
Now we gotta go hang out
at the grocery store again.

2296
01:27:18,290 --> 01:27:19,954
- [Tom] (yawns)

2297
01:27:19,954 --> 01:27:20,995
- [Joel] I feel empty.

2298
01:27:20,995 --> 01:27:23,162
Kind of a letdown, really.

2299
01:27:25,306 --> 01:27:28,906
- [Tom] But for the lowly detonator...

2300
01:27:28,906 --> 01:27:30,268
- [Joel] Oh, lost your head.

2301
01:27:30,268 --> 01:27:32,209
- [Tom] My head! Grab it!

2302
01:27:32,209 --> 01:27:35,410
- [Crow] Gotta figure out
how this is gonna end here.

2303
01:27:35,410 --> 01:27:37,993
(low growling)

2304
01:27:45,623 --> 01:27:46,523
- There we go.

2305
01:27:46,523 --> 01:27:48,070
- Joel, I can't help but feel

2306
01:27:48,070 --> 01:27:50,306
that that film was flawed in certain ways.

2307
01:27:50,306 --> 01:27:51,740
- What do you mean by that?

2308
01:27:51,740 --> 01:27:52,573
- I don't know.

2309
01:27:52,573 --> 01:27:54,471
I guess I just felt that Corman failed

2310
01:27:54,471 --> 01:27:56,742
to deliver on concepts and ideas promised

2311
01:27:56,742 --> 01:27:59,763
as in films like Viking
Women and the Sea Serpent.

2312
01:27:59,763 --> 01:28:01,456
- You know, I had problems
with it, too, Joel.

2313
01:28:01,456 --> 01:28:03,357
I can't figure out if
those leech creatures

2314
01:28:03,357 --> 01:28:05,763
were smart or just big dumb monsters.

2315
01:28:05,763 --> 01:28:08,514
- Well, they were smart
enough to take hostages.

2316
01:28:08,514 --> 01:28:10,042
- Yeah, that's right.

2317
01:28:10,042 --> 01:28:12,525
When that drunk poacher
fingered one of the leeches,

2318
01:28:12,525 --> 01:28:14,961
they sent a contract man out to kill him.

2319
01:28:14,961 --> 01:28:18,392
- Yeah, so maybe they kept
everybody busy at the swamp

2320
01:28:18,392 --> 01:28:21,325
while they went into town and
stole everybody's antiques.

2321
01:28:21,325 --> 01:28:23,053
- I don't know if they were that smart.

2322
01:28:23,053 --> 01:28:25,140
I mean, let's remember
they did get caught.

2323
01:28:25,140 --> 01:28:27,330
I think they just went
on killing and killing

2324
01:28:27,330 --> 01:28:29,684
until it was all about killing

2325
01:28:29,684 --> 01:28:32,141
and it wasn't about fun anymore.

2326
01:28:32,141 --> 01:28:33,921
- Knowing what I know,

2327
01:28:33,921 --> 01:28:36,661
I guess that they're
smarter than a sea turtle

2328
01:28:36,661 --> 01:28:39,707
but dumber than Danish
physicist Niels Bohr.

2329
01:28:39,707 --> 01:28:41,054
- Yeah, like I can see them going

2330
01:28:41,054 --> 01:28:42,806
to four of the Police Academy movies

2331
01:28:42,806 --> 01:28:44,225
before they stopped shelling out the dough

2332
01:28:44,225 --> 01:28:46,348
and waiting for them to
come out on Showtime.

2333
01:28:46,348 --> 01:28:48,744
- They're the kind of
leeches that would probably

2334
01:28:48,744 --> 01:28:51,602
think of Chili's as their
favorite restaurant,

2335
01:28:51,602 --> 01:28:53,207
and it would never really dawn on them

2336
01:28:53,207 --> 01:28:54,563
that it was a chain.

2337
01:28:54,563 --> 01:28:56,604
- Or they'd be the type
to watch paid programming

2338
01:28:56,604 --> 01:28:58,697
and never figure out that it was an ad.

2339
01:28:58,697 --> 01:28:59,962
(laughs)

2340
01:28:59,962 --> 01:29:01,223
- Well this is a lot of fun, you guys,

2341
01:29:01,223 --> 01:29:03,096
but I think it's time to read a letter.

2342
01:29:03,096 --> 01:29:05,580
Let's put this up on still store, Cambot.

2343
01:29:05,580 --> 01:29:07,580
This one comes from a...

2344
01:29:09,426 --> 01:29:12,557
Jean A. Dawn, and she writes,

2345
01:29:12,557 --> 01:29:16,113
"Dear Joel, I am positively
crazy about your show.

2346
01:29:16,113 --> 01:29:19,000
"When I can find it on TV,
I tape it and play it over

2347
01:29:19,000 --> 01:29:22,661
"so I don't miss any of
your sarcastic remarks.

2348
01:29:22,661 --> 01:29:24,995
"I keep recommending your
show to everyone I can.

2349
01:29:24,995 --> 01:29:27,996
"Everyone who is lucky
to catch it loves it.

2350
01:29:27,996 --> 01:29:30,155
"PS: I'm 59 years old, and I've never

2351
01:29:30,155 --> 01:29:31,804
"written a fan letter in my life."

2352
01:29:31,804 --> 01:29:34,221
Well thank you, Jean A. Dawn.

2353
01:29:35,767 --> 01:29:37,855
- Oh, bless you Jean.

2354
01:29:37,855 --> 01:29:39,226
- Jean A. Dawn.

2355
01:29:39,226 --> 01:29:40,554
- Nice life goal.

2356
01:29:40,554 --> 01:29:41,956
- Jeaninging.

2357
01:29:41,956 --> 01:29:43,335
What do you think, sirs?

2358
01:29:43,335 --> 01:29:46,982
(slurping sounds)

2359
01:29:46,982 --> 01:29:48,215
- Oh, sorry, Frank.

2360
01:29:48,215 --> 01:29:50,184
I left the leech on too long.

2361
01:29:50,184 --> 01:29:51,143
(pop)

2362
01:29:51,143 --> 01:29:52,831
Well, Frank...

2363
01:29:52,831 --> 01:29:54,252
Frank?

2364
01:29:54,252 --> 01:29:56,682
Well, now that you've sucked
all the blood out of Frank,

2365
01:29:56,682 --> 01:29:57,667
what are you gonna do now?

2366
01:29:57,667 --> 01:30:00,462
- Oh, write, produce,
be as creative as I can.

2367
01:30:00,462 --> 01:30:02,912
- Have you thought about
the mad scientist game?

2368
01:30:02,912 --> 01:30:04,577
- Ha, don't get me started.

2369
01:30:04,577 --> 01:30:05,437
You know, I got some ideas.

2370
01:30:05,437 --> 01:30:06,826
Maybe we can do lunch?

2371
01:30:06,826 --> 01:30:07,659
- Sure!

2372
01:30:07,659 --> 01:30:09,063
- You think you could pick it up?

2373
01:30:09,063 --> 01:30:10,550
I'm, uh, flat busted.

2374
01:30:10,550 --> 01:30:12,346
- You know, I'd really
love to produce you.

2375
01:30:12,346 --> 01:30:16,303
I had this concept of
maybe we could go into...

2376
01:30:16,303 --> 01:30:17,972
(power down)

2377
01:30:17,972 --> 01:30:20,805
(keyboard music)

